{"u":"Chapter 8: Fluids and Electrolytes","q":[{"i":1,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is reviewing laboratory concepts for a newly admitted client. Which substance does the nurse identify as an electrolyte that dissociates into electrically charged ions when dissolved in body fluids?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Glucose","Glucose remains intact as a complex molecule and does not dissociate into ions in solution."],["B","Sodium chloride","Sodium chloride separates into positively and negatively charged ions when dissolved in body fluid solutions."],["C","Urea","Urea is a non-electrolyte waste solute that does not split into charged particles in solution."],["D","Creatinine","Creatinine remains a whole molecule in body fluids rather than ionizing into electrically charged atoms."]],"a":["B"],"obj":"Electrolytes ionize into charged particles in solution, whereas glucose, urea, and creatinine do not dissociate.","fc":["Electrolytes vs. Non-Electrolytes","Electrolytes ionize into charged ions in solution | Sodium chloride dissociates into separate ions | Glucose, urea, and creatinine do not dissociate","Section I.A: Electrolytes (pp. 88-89)"]},{"i":2,"t":"m","d":2,"c":6,"j":2,"st":"A nurse prepares to administer intravenous electrolyte replacement and reviews the ordered milliequivalents (mEq). Which principle accurately describes the chemical combining activity of electrolytes?","ex":null,"o":[["A","One mEq of a cation always reacts chemically with one mEq of an anion.","Milliequivalents measure chemical combining power, where one mEq of any cation reacts with one mEq of an anion."],["B","One mEq measures the total molecular weight of an uncharged solute in liters.","Milliequivalents express chemical combining activity of ions rather than molecular weight of uncharged solutes."],["C","Two mEq of a cation are required to neutralize one mEq of an anion.","Cations and anions combine in an exact one-to-one milliequivalent ratio to maintain electrical neutrality."],["D","Milliequivalents indicate the volume of fluid needed to dilute an intravenous medication.","Fluid volumes are measured in metric liters or milliliters, while milliequivalents measure ionic combining activity."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"One milliequivalent of any cation always reacts chemically with one milliequivalent of an anion.","fc":["Milliequivalent (mEq) Principle","Measures combining activity of electrolytes | 1 mEq of any cation reacts with 1 mEq of an anion | Fluid volume uses liters (L) or milliliters (mL)","Section I.A: Electrolytes (p. 88)"]},{"i":3,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is teaching a nursing student about the electrical properties of electrolytes and their atomic components. Which statements by the student indicate accurate understanding? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","A cation is an ion that has lost electrons and carries a positive electrical charge.","Losing negatively charged electrons leaves more protons than electrons, giving a cation a positive charge."],["B","An anion has gained electrons and therefore carries a negative electrical charge.","Gaining additional electrons gives an atom an excess of negative charges, creating a negatively charged anion."],["C","An atom with an equal number of protons and electrons carries a net positive charge.","Equal numbers of positively charged protons and negatively charged electrons result in a neutral atom."],["D","The nucleus of an atom is positively charged because it contains protons and neutrons.","Protons carry a positive charge and neutrons are neutral, making the atomic nucleus positively charged."],["E","Electrons carry a positive charge and remain fixed inside the nucleus of the atom.","Electrons carry a negative electrical charge and revolve continuously around the positively charged nucleus."]],"a":["A","B","D"],"obj":"Cations lose electrons to become positive, anions gain electrons to become negative, and nuclei are positive.","fc":["Atomic & Ionic Properties (Box 8.1)","Cation: lost electrons, positive charge | Anion: gained electrons, negative charge | Nucleus: protons (+) and neutrons (neutral)","Box 8.1: Properties of Electrolytes (p. 89)"]},{"i":4,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"When analyzing body fluid distribution in an average adult client, which compartment does the nurse identify as containing the largest percentage of total body fluid?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Interstitial fluid compartment","Interstitial fluid accounts for approximately 22% of total adult body fluid within the extracellular space."],["B","Intravascular fluid compartment","Intravascular fluid inside blood vessels comprises only about 6% of total adult body fluid."],["C","Intracellular fluid compartment","Intracellular fluid inside the body cells makes up approximately 70% of total adult body fluid."],["D","Transcellular fluid compartment","Transcellular fluid in specialized spaces accounts for only about 2% of total adult body fluid."]],"a":["C"],"obj":"In the average adult, intracellular fluid accounts for 70% of body fluid and extracellular fluid for 30%.","fc":["Adult Fluid Compartment Distribution (Fig. 8.1)","Intracellular fluid (ICF): 70% | Extracellular fluid (ECF): 30% | ECF breakdown: 22% interstitial, 6% intravascular, 2% transcellular","Fig. 8.1: Fluid Compartments (p. 89)"]},{"i":5,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is reviewing a client's chart noting an abnormality in transcellular fluid volume. Which body fluid does the nurse recognize as part of the transcellular compartment?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Plasma within the femoral artery","Plasma inside blood vessels belongs to the intravascular compartment rather than the transcellular fluid space."],["B","Fluid inside skeletal muscle cells","Fluid contained within the walls of body cells forms the intracellular fluid compartment."],["C","Cerebrospinal fluid within spinal canals","Transcellular fluid includes specialized fluids in cerebrospinal canals, lymphatic tissues, synovial joints, and the eye."],["D","Fluid filling spaces directly between tissue cells","Fluid located directly in the spaces between cells is classified as interstitial fluid."]],"a":["C"],"obj":"Transcellular fluid comprises 2% of adult fluid and includes cerebrospinal, lymphatic, synovial, and ocular fluids.","fc":["Transcellular Fluid Compartment","Represents 2% of total adult body fluid | Includes cerebrospinal canals and synovial joints | Also includes lymphatic tissues and eye fluid","Fig. 8.1: Fluid Compartments (p. 89)"]},{"i":6,"t":"s","d":3,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A nurse is caring for a client who sustained extensive thermal burns and is at risk for third-spacing. Which clinical concepts apply to third-spacing? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Trapped extracellular fluid is unavailable for normal physiological processes.","Sequestered fluid in third-spacing cannot circulate or participate in normal cellular and vascular perfusion."],["B","Daily weight measurements reliably detect early intravascular loss from third-spacing.","Because fluid remains trapped inside the body, total body weight may not reflect intravascular depletion."],["C","Fluid can become sequestered in the peritoneal, pleural, or pericardial cavities.","Third-spacing traps extracellular fluid in potential body cavities, bowel, abdomen, or injured soft tissues."],["D","Intake and output records always show an immediate negative balance during third-spacing.","Standard intake and output records often fail to capture fluid shifted into nonfunctional third spaces."],["E","Intravascular depletion from third-spacing may remain hidden until organ malfunction occurs.","Clinical recognition of intravascular loss is difficult and often delayed until hypoperfusion causes organ dysfunction."]],"a":["A","C","E"],"obj":"Third-spacing sequesters extracellular fluid in body spaces, masking intravascular loss from weights and intake/output records.","fc":["Third-Spacing Concepts","Trapped ECF unavailable for normal physiology | Sites: peritoneal, pleural, pericardial, bowel, burns | Often undetected by weight or I&O until organ malfunction","Section I.C: Third-Spacing (p. 88)"]},{"i":7,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A client admitted with decompensated cirrhosis and heart failure presents with severe generalized edema throughout all body tissues. Which medical term does the nurse use to document this finding?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Anasarca","Anasarca is generalized interstitial edema throughout the body caused by cardiac, renal, or liver failure."],["B","Third-spacing","Third-spacing describes fluid trapped in potential body cavities rather than generalized interstitial tissue swelling."],["C","Localized edema","Localized edema is confined to a specific site of trauma, surgery, burns, or local inflammation."],["D","Hypovolemia","Hypovolemia refers to an isotonic decrease in circulating intravascular blood volume rather than widespread tissue swelling."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Anasarca is generalized interstitial edema caused by cardiac, renal, or hepatic failure, unlike localized traumatic edema.","fc":["Edema Classifications","Localized edema: trauma, surgery, inflammation, burns | Generalized edema (anasarca): widespread interstitial fluid | Anasarca causes: cardiac, renal, or liver failure","Section I.D: Edema (pp. 88-89)"]},{"i":8,"t":"m","d":5,"c":3,"j":3,"st":"The nurse is triaging four clients in an urgent care clinic during a summer heatwave. Based on age-related total body fluid percentages, which client requires the highest priority monitoring for rapid fluid imbalance?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A 6-month-old infant with mild vomiting","Infants have 80% total body water and lose fluid rapidly, placing them at highest risk."],["B","A 28-year-old marathon runner with muscle fatigue","Young adults have approximately 60% body water and possess greater physiological reserve against acute fluid shifts."],["C","A 42-year-old office worker with mild headache","Middle-aged adults maintain roughly 60% body water and tolerate minor fluid fluctuations better than infants."],["D","A 78-year-old retired teacher with decreased thirst","Older adults have only 55% body water and high risk, though infants carry highest fluid turnover."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Total body fluid is 80% in infants, 60% in adults, and 55% in older adults, making infants and older adults vulnerable.","fc":["Body Fluid by Age","Infant: 80% of body weight | Adult: 60% of body weight | Older adult: 55% of body weight (least reserve)","Section I.E: Body Fluid (p. 89)"]},{"i":9,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is reviewing the anatomical distribution of extracellular fluid in the human body. Which components are included within the extracellular fluid compartment? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Interstitial fluid between cells","Interstitial fluid surrounds tissue cells and forms the largest portion of the extracellular fluid compartment."],["B","Intravascular blood plasma","Blood within the vascular system is a primary component of the extracellular fluid compartment."],["C","Fluid inside red blood cells","Fluid contained within the cell membranes of erythrocytes belongs to the intracellular fluid compartment."],["D","Lymph and transcellular fluid","Lymphatic fluid and transcellular secretions are classified within the extracellular fluid compartment."],["E","Fluid within bone and connective tissue","Water within dense connective tissue and bone matrix forms part of the extracellular fluid compartment."]],"a":["A","B","D","E"],"obj":"Extracellular fluid includes interstitial, intravascular blood, lymph, bone, connective tissue, water, and transcellular fluid.","fc":["Extracellular Fluid (ECF) Components","Fluid located outside body cells | Includes interstitial fluid, blood, and lymph | Includes bone, connective tissue, water, transcellular fluid","Section I.B: Body Fluid Compartments (p. 88)"]},{"i":10,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse is explaining cellular electrolyte homeostasis to a nursing student. What occurs at the cellular membrane when a positively charged electrolyte moves out of a body cell?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Another electrolyte moves into the cell to maintain equal numbers of cations and anions.","Electroneutrality requires equal cations and anions, so another electrolyte enters whenever one exits a cell."],["B","The cell membrane becomes completely impermeable to prevent further ion movement.","Cell membranes remain selectively permeable and continuously exchange ions to preserve electrical and chemical homeostasis."],["C","Intracellular water immediately exits the cell to create a net negative charge.","Water moves according to osmotic gradients, whereas ion exchange preserves balanced positive and negative charges."],["D","Non-electrolytes like glucose ionize to replace the lost intracellular cations.","Glucose does not ionize in solution and cannot replace lost electrically charged cations inside the cell."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Whenever an electrolyte exits a cell, another enters so cation and anion numbers stay equal for homeostasis.","fc":["Cellular Electrolyte Homeostasis","Compartments separated by semipermeable membranes | Electrolyte exiting cell is replaced by another | Equal cations and anions required for homeostasis","Section I.B: Body Fluid Compartments (p. 88)"]},{"i":11,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"During a physiology review, a student asks how dissolved electrolytes spread evenly within a single fluid compartment. Which transport process does the nurse describe?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Osmosis","Osmosis is the movement of water solvent across a semipermeable membrane toward higher solute concentration."],["B","Diffusion","Diffusion moves dissolved solutes from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration."],["C","Filtration","Filtration pushes both water and solutes across a membrane using a hydrostatic pressure gradient."],["D","Active transport","Active transport uses cellular metabolic energy to pump ions against a concentration or osmotic gradient."]],"a":["B"],"obj":"Diffusion is the passive movement of dissolved solutes from an area of higher to lower concentration.","fc":["Diffusion Transport Mechanism","Solute spreads through a solution or solvent | Moves from higher to lower solute concentration | Occurs across permeable or selectively permeable membranes","Section I.F: Body Fluid Transport (p. 89)"]},{"i":12,"t":"m","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client receives an intravenous solution that creates a higher solute concentration in the intravascular space than inside the cells. How does water move in response to this gradient?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Water moves by osmosis from the lower solute concentration inside cells to the higher concentration in plasma.","Osmosis pulls solvent across a selectively permeable membrane from a less concentrated solution into a more concentrated one."],["B","Solutes move by osmosis from the plasma into the intracellular fluid to equalize pressure.","Osmosis refers specifically to solvent water movement rather than solute movement across a semipermeable membrane."],["C","Water moves by filtration from the low-pressure intracellular space into the high-pressure artery.","Filtration moves fluid from higher to lower hydrostatic pressure rather than following solute concentration gradients."],["D","Water remains static because cell membranes are completely impermeable to solvent molecules.","Cell membranes are selectively permeable to water, allowing osmotic shifts whenever solute concentrations differ across membranes."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Osmosis moves solvent molecules across a selectively permeable membrane from lower to higher solute concentration.","fc":["Osmosis & Osmotic Pressure","Movement of solvent (water) across semipermeable membrane | Moves from lower to higher solute concentration | Osmotic pressure pulls water toward concentrated solute","Section I.F: Body Fluid Transport (p. 90)"]},{"i":13,"t":"s","d":1,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is reviewing capillary fluid exchange dynamics at the arterial and venous ends of a capillary bed. Which physiological events occur during normal capillary filtration? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","At the arterial end, hydrostatic pressure exceeds osmotic pressure, pushing fluid out of the capillary.","Higher arterial hydrostatic pressure forces water and diffusible solutes out into the interstitial space by filtration."],["B","At the venous end, osmotic pressure exceeds hydrostatic pressure, drawing fluid back into the capillary.","Higher venous osmotic pull draws interstitial water and solutes back into the intravascular capillary compartment."],["C","Excess fluid and solutes remaining in interstitial spaces return to circulation via lymph channels.","Lymphatic channels collect residual interstitial fluid and proteins and return them to the intravascular compartment."],["D","Hydrostatic pressure pulls water from areas of lower pressure into areas of higher pressure.","Hydrostatic pressure is the weight of a solution pushing fluid from higher to lower pressure."],["E","At the arterial end, osmotic pressure exceeds hydrostatic pressure, pulling interstitial fluid inward.","Arterial hydrostatic pressure is higher than osmotic pressure, resulting in outward filtration rather than inward pull."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Arterial hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out, venous osmotic pressure pulls fluid in, and lymphatics drain excess.","fc":["Capillary Filtration & Hydrostatic Pressure","Arterial end: hydrostatic > osmotic (fluid moves out) | Venous end: osmotic > hydrostatic (fluid moves in) | Lymph channels return excess interstitial fluid","Section I.F: Body Fluid Transport (p. 90)"]},{"i":14,"t":"m","d":5,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A nurse reviews the laboratory results of an older adult client admitted with confusion and poor oral intake. Which serum osmolality value indicates hyperosmolality requiring further fluid volume evaluation?","ex":{"t":"Laboratory Results","r":[["Serum Osmolality","312 mOsm/kg"],["Serum Potassium","4.1 mEq/L"]]},"o":[["A","312 mOsm/kg","Normal plasma osmolality is 275 to 295 mOsm/kg, so 312 mOsm/kg indicates elevated solute concentration."],["B","285 mOsm/kg","A value of 285 mOsm/kg falls squarely within the normal plasma osmolality range of 275 to 295 mOsm/kg."],["C","276 mOsm/kg","A value of 276 mOsm/kg is at the lower end of normal plasma osmolality (275 to 295 mOsm/kg)."],["D","260 mOsm/kg","A value of 260 mOsm/kg falls below normal plasma osmolality and indicates hypo-osmolality or fluid excess."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Normal plasma osmolality is 275 to 295 mOsm/kg (275 to 295 mmol/kg).","fc":["Plasma Osmolality","Measures osmotically active particles per kg of water | Normal range: 275 to 295 mOsm/kg | Measured in milliosmoles (mOsm)","Section I.F: Body Fluid Transport (p. 90)"]},{"i":15,"t":"m","d":4,"c":6,"j":2,"st":"A client is prescribed a hypotonic intravenous infusion to treat cellular dehydration. How does a hypotonic solution affect fluid movement across red blood cell membranes?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Water moves into the cells by osmosis because the solution has lower osmolality than body fluids.","Hypotonic solutions contain fewer solutes than body fluids, causing water to enter cells by osmosis."],["B","Water moves out of the cells into the plasma, causing the cells to shrink.","Cell shrinkage occurs when hypertonic solutions draw intracellular water out into the more concentrated extracellular space."],["C","No net water movement occurs because the solution matches normal plasma osmolality.","Equilibrium without net osmosis occurs when isotonic solutions matching body fluid osmolality are administered."],["D","Solutes rapidly diffuse out of the cells until the extracellular fluid becomes hypertonic.","Hypotonic solutions act primarily by shifting water inward via osmosis rather than exporting cellular solutes."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypotonic solutions have lower osmolality than body fluids, driving water into cells via osmosis.","fc":["Solution Tonicity Effects","Isotonic: equal osmolality, minimal osmosis | Hypotonic: lower solute/osmolality, water enters cells | Hypertonic: higher solute/osmolality, water leaves cells","Section I.G: Movement of Body Fluid (p. 90)"]},{"i":16,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is teaching a physiology class about active transport across cell membranes. Which statements accurately characterize active transport? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Active transport moves ions from an area of lower concentration to higher concentration.","Active transport pumps molecules against concentration and osmotic gradients rather than relying on passive diffusion."],["B","Cellular metabolic processes supply the energy required for active transport systems.","Metabolic energy within the cell powers active transport pumps across selectively permeable cell membranes."],["C","Sodium, potassium, calcium, iron, and hydrogen ions are moved via active transport.","Essential ions including sodium, potassium, calcium, iron, and hydrogen rely on active transport mechanisms."],["D","Sugars and amino acids can also be transported actively across cell membranes.","In addition to ions, certain sugars and amino acids utilize active transport to enter cells."],["E","Active transport occurs passively whenever hydrostatic pressure rises inside capillaries.","Hydrostatic pressure drives passive filtration, whereas active transport requires cellular energy against concentration gradients."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Active transport uses cellular metabolic energy to move Na, K, Ca, Fe, H, sugars, and amino acids against gradients.","fc":["Active Transport","Moves ions against concentration and osmotic pressure | Powered by cellular metabolic energy | Transports Na, K, Ca, Fe, H, sugars, amino acids","Section I.G: Movement of Body Fluid (p. 90)"]},{"i":17,"t":"m","d":2,"c":5,"j":2,"st":"A nurse is calculating total daily fluid intake for a client consuming a 2,000-calorie diet with normal oral hydration. Approximately how much water is produced daily through metabolic oxidation of food?","ex":null,"o":[["A","300 mL","Metabolic oxidation produces about 10 mL per 100 calories, totaling approximately 300 mL daily."],["B","1,500 mL","Approximately 1,200 to 1,500 mL of daily fluid intake comes from orally ingested water and liquids."],["C","900 mL","Approximately 800 to 1,100 mL of daily fluid intake is obtained from water contained in solid foods."],["D","50 mL","Metabolic oxidation of fats, carbohydrates, and proteins yields roughly 300 mL daily, far exceeding 50 mL."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Daily fluid intake totals 2,300-2,900 mL from liquids (1,200-1,500 mL), food (800-1,100 mL), and oxidation (300 mL).","fc":["Daily Fluid Intake Sources (Fig. 8.2)","Ingested water: 1,200-1,500 mL | Ingested food: 800-1,100 mL | Metabolic oxidation: 300 mL (~10 mL per 100 kcal)","Fig. 8.2: Fluid Intake and Output (p. 91)"]},{"i":18,"t":"m","d":4,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A client is admitted with a temperature of 103.2 F (39.6 C) and a respiratory rate of 32 breaths/min. Which physiological fluid loss increases significantly in this client?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Insensible water loss through the skin and lungs","Fever accelerates perspiration through skin, while rapid respirations increase water vapor loss from the lungs."],["B","Sensible fluid excretion through the gastrointestinal tract","Normal gastrointestinal stool loss is approximately 100 mL daily and does not rise solely from tachypnea."],["C","Metabolic water formation from cellular oxidation","Elevated temperature increases insensible fluid losses rather than generating compensatory metabolic water intake."],["D","Lymphatic fluid drainage into the thoracic duct","Fever and hyperventilation directly increase evaporative insensible losses from skin and respiratory mucosa."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Insensible water loss occurs through skin (600-800 mL) and lungs (400-600 mL) and rises with fever and tachypnea.","fc":["Insensible Fluid Losses (Fig. 8.2)","Skin loss: 600-800 mL (varies with body/ambient temp) | Lung loss: 400-600 mL (varies with rate/depth of breathing) | Unaware and unmeasured directly","Section I.H & Fig. 8.2 (p. 91)"]},{"i":19,"t":"s","d":5,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is evaluating endocrine and renal mechanisms that maintain fluid and electrolyte homeostasis in a client experiencing blood loss. Which compensatory actions occur? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","The adrenal glands secrete aldosterone to increase renal reabsorption of sodium.","Aldosterone from the adrenal glands promotes kidney sodium reabsorption to help expand extracellular fluid volume."],["B","The pituitary gland releases antidiuretic hormone to increase renal water reabsorption.","Antidiuretic hormone from the pituitary gland regulates extracellular osmotic pressure by increasing renal water reabsorption."],["C","The kidneys adjust the quantity of water and electrolytes excreted in urine.","Normal kidneys play the primary role in regulating fluid and electrolyte balance by adjusting excretion."],["D","The pituitary gland secretes aldosterone to promote potassium excretion in stool.","Aldosterone is secreted by the adrenal glands rather than the pituitary and acts on the kidneys."],["E","The adrenal glands release antidiuretic hormone to stimulate gastrointestinal water secretion.","Antidiuretic hormone is released by the pituitary gland and acts on renal tubules rather than the bowel."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Kidneys regulate fluid balance alongside adrenal aldosterone (sodium reabsorption) and pituitary ADH (water reabsorption).","fc":["Organ & Hormonal Fluid Regulation","Kidneys: primary regulator of water/electrolyte excretion | Adrenal aldosterone: regulates renal sodium reabsorption | Pituitary ADH: regulates renal water reabsorption","Section II: Maintaining Balance (p. 91)"]},{"i":20,"t":"m","d":3,"c":7,"j":3,"st":"A nurse is reviewing the care plan for four clients on a medical-surgical unit. Based on gastrointestinal fluid dynamics, which client is at highest risk for developing an acute fluid and electrolyte imbalance?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A client with severe infectious diarrhea for 48 hours","Large volumes of electrolyte-rich GI secretions are normally reabsorbed, so severe diarrhea causes massive fluid/electrolyte loss."],["B","A client who is 1 day postoperative from cataract surgery","Uncomplicated eye surgery does not disrupt gastrointestinal fluid reabsorption or cause significant electrolyte loss."],["C","A client with controlled hypertension taking daily lisinopril","Stable antihypertensive therapy without vomiting or diarrhea does not cause acute gastrointestinal fluid depletion."],["D","A client scheduled for a routine screening colonoscopy tomorrow","Before bowel preparation begins, a client awaiting screening has intact gastrointestinal fluid absorption."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Most fluid secreted into the GI tract is normally reabsorbed, making severe diarrhea a high risk for imbalance.","fc":["Gastrointestinal Fluid Balance","Large electrolyte-rich volumes enter GI tract daily | Almost all GI fluid is normally reabsorbed | Severe diarrhea causes rapid fluid and electrolyte loss","Section I.H: Body Fluid Intake and Output (p. 91)"]},{"i":21,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A client arrives in the emergency department after acute hemorrhage from a laceration, losing water and electrolytes in equal proportions. Which type of dehydration does the nurse recognize?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Isotonic dehydration","Isotonic dehydration (hypovolemia) involves equal loss of water and electrolytes and is the most common type."],["B","Hypertonic dehydration","Hypertonic dehydration occurs when water loss exceeds electrolyte loss, causing cellular dehydration and shrinkage."],["C","Hypotonic dehydration","Hypotonic dehydration occurs when electrolyte loss exceeds water loss, causing fluid to shift into cells."],["D","Hypotonic overhydration","Hypotonic overhydration represents water intoxication with fluid excess rather than acute intravascular volume depletion."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Isotonic dehydration (hypovolemia) is the most common type, where water and electrolytes are lost in equal proportions.","fc":["Isotonic Dehydration (Hypovolemia)","Water and dissolved electrolytes lost in equal proportions | Most common type of dehydration | Causes decreased circulating blood volume and poor perfusion","Section III.B: Fluid Volume Deficit (p. 92)"]},{"i":22,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with uncontrolled diabetes insipidus loses large amounts of free water exceeding electrolyte loss. What fluid shift occurs between body compartments in hypertonic dehydration?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Fluid shifts from the intracellular compartment into the plasma and interstitial spaces, shrinking cells.","Higher extracellular solute concentration draws water out of cells by osmosis, causing cellular dehydration and shrinkage."],["B","Fluid shifts from the intravascular space into the intracellular compartment, causing cells to swell.","Cellular swelling occurs in hypotonic dehydration when extracellular fluid becomes less concentrated than intracellular fluid."],["C","Fluid remains equally distributed with no osmotic movement across cell membranes.","Unequal water loss raises extracellular osmolality, creating an osmotic gradient that pulls water out of cells."],["D","Fluid shifts exclusively into the transcellular space, causing generalized anasarca.","Hypertonic dehydration depletes intracellular fluid rather than causing generalized interstitial edema or anasarca."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"In hypertonic dehydration, water loss exceeds electrolyte loss, pulling fluid out of cells and causing cellular shrinkage.","fc":["Hypertonic Dehydration","Water loss exceeds electrolyte loss | Fluid moves from ICF into plasma and interstitial spaces | Results in cellular dehydration and shrinkage","Section III.B: Fluid Volume Deficit (p. 92)"]},{"i":23,"t":"s","d":3,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is assessing clients for risk factors associated with hypertonic dehydration. Which clinical conditions predispose a client to hypertonic dehydration? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Prolonged high fever and excessive perspiration","Sustained fever and diaphoresis cause greater loss of free water than electrolytes, leading to hypertonic dehydration."],["B","Diabetic ketoacidosis and diabetes insipidus","Both ketoacidosis and diabetes insipidus promote excessive free water loss relative to electrolyte excretion."],["C","Sustained hyperventilation","Rapid, deep breathing increases insensible free water loss through expired air without losing electrolytes."],["D","Excessive hypotonic intravenous fluid replacement","Excessive hypotonic fluid replacement dilutes extracellular electrolytes and causes hypotonic dehydration or water intoxication."],["E","Chronic malnutrition with low solute intake","Chronic malnutrition typically contributes to hypotonic dehydration where electrolyte loss exceeds water loss."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hypertonic dehydration is caused by fever, diaphoresis, hyperventilation, ketoacidosis, diarrhea, early CKD, and diabetes insipidus.","fc":["Causes of Hypertonic Dehydration","Excessive perspiration, prolonged fever, hyperventilation | Ketoacidosis and diabetes insipidus | Diarrhea and early-stage kidney disease","Section III.C: Fluid Volume Deficit (p. 92)"]},{"i":24,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with chronic malnutrition and kidney disease develops hypotonic dehydration. How does this fluid imbalance affect plasma volume and cellular hydration?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Electrolyte loss exceeds water loss, causing fluid to shift into cells, reducing plasma volume and swelling cells.","Lower extracellular solute concentration drives water into cells by osmosis, depleting intravascular plasma volume."],["B","Water loss exceeds electrolyte loss, pulling water out of cells and expanding intravascular plasma volume.","Water loss exceeding electrolyte loss describes hypertonic dehydration, which shrinks cells rather than swelling them."],["C","Water and electrolytes are lost equally, leaving intracellular cell size completely unchanged.","Equal loss describes isotonic dehydration, whereas hypotonic dehydration produces osmotic shifts into body cells."],["D","Excessive sodium retention expands both plasma volume and intracellular fluid volume equally.","Hypotonic dehydration is a volume deficit state caused by electrolyte loss rather than sodium retention."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"In hypotonic dehydration, electrolyte loss exceeds water loss, shifting fluid into cells and causing plasma deficit and cell swelling.","fc":["Hypotonic Dehydration","Electrolyte loss exceeds water loss | Fluid shifts from plasma/interstitial spaces into cells | Causes plasma volume deficit and cellular swelling","Section III.B: Fluid Volume Deficit (p. 92)"]},{"i":25,"t":"m","d":1,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"The nurse is reviewing prescribed intravenous fluid orders for three dehydrated clients. Which pairing of dehydration type and intravenous fluid therapy reflects standard clinical management?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hypertonic dehydration treated with a hypotonic intravenous solution","Hypotonic solutions provide free water to lower elevated extracellular osmolality and rehydrate shrunken cells."],["B","Isotonic dehydration treated with a hypertonic intravenous solution","Isotonic dehydration requires isotonic solutions to restore intravascular volume without causing osmotic cellular shifts."],["C","Hypotonic dehydration treated with a hypotonic intravenous solution","Administering hypotonic fluids in hypotonic dehydration worsens extracellular dilution and dangerous cellular swelling."],["D","Hypertonic dehydration treated with a hypertonic intravenous solution","Hypertonic fluids worsen extracellular hyperosmolality and accelerate cellular dehydration in hypertonic dehydration."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Isotonic dehydration is treated with isotonic fluids, hypertonic with hypotonic fluids, and hypotonic with hypertonic fluids.","fc":["IV Fluid Selection for Dehydration","Isotonic dehydration -> isotonic IV fluids | Hypertonic dehydration -> hypotonic IV fluids | Hypotonic dehydration -> hypertonic IV fluids","Section III.E: Fluid Volume Deficit (p. 92)"]},{"i":26,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A nurse assesses a client admitted with severe fluid volume deficit. Which cardiovascular and renal findings does the nurse expect to observe? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Thready, increased pulse rate with diminished peripheral pulses","Compensatory sympathetic activation increases heart rate while reduced stroke volume produces weak, thready pulses."],["B","Orthostatic hypotension and flat neck veins in dependent positions","Decreased intravascular volume lowers blood pressure upon standing and prevents venous distention."],["C","Decreased central venous pressure and decreased urine output","Reduced right atrial filling lowers central venous pressure while kidneys conserve water, reducing urine output."],["D","Bounding pulse with distended neck and hand veins","Bounding pulses and venous distention indicate intravascular fluid volume excess rather than fluid deficit."],["E","Elevated central venous pressure with elevated blood pressure","High central venous pressure and hypertension reflect hypervolemia rather than intravascular volume depletion."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Fluid volume deficit presents with thready tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, flat neck veins, low CVP, and oliguria.","fc":["Fluid Volume Deficit: CV & Renal (Table 8.1)","Thready rapid pulse, orthostatic hypotension | Flat neck/hand veins, decreased CVP | Decreased urine output (renal water conservation)","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":27,"t":"m","d":2,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A nurse reviews the admission laboratory panel for a client with suspected severe dehydration. Which combination of laboratory results supports a diagnosis of fluid volume deficit?","ex":{"t":"Admission Lab Panel","r":[["Serum Osmolality","308 mOsm/kg"],["Hematocrit","54%"],["BUN","34 mg/dL"],["Urine Specific Gravity","1.035"]]},"o":[["A","Increased serum osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, serum sodium, and urine specific gravity","Hemoconcentration and renal water conservation elevate osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, sodium, and urine specific gravity."],["B","Decreased serum osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, serum sodium, and urine specific gravity","Dilutional decreases across these laboratory values occur in fluid volume excess rather than fluid volume deficit."],["C","Increased hematocrit and BUN with decreased serum osmolality and urine specific gravity","Dehydration concentrates both blood and urine, elevating serum osmolality and urinary specific gravity together."],["D","Decreased hematocrit with increased urine output and low urinary specific gravity","Low hematocrit and dilute polyuria reflect overhydration or diuresis rather than fluid volume deficit."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Fluid volume deficit elevates serum osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, serum sodium, and urinary specific gravity.","fc":["Fluid Volume Deficit: Labs (Table 8.1)","Increased serum osmolality and serum sodium | Increased hematocrit and BUN (hemoconcentration) | Increased urinary specific gravity (concentrated urine)","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":28,"t":"m","d":5,"c":8,"j":3,"st":"A nurse is caring for a client experiencing an acute fluid and electrolyte imbalance. Which comprehensive systems assessment must the nurse perform to detect early physiological deterioration?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Cardiovascular, respiratory, neurological, musculoskeletal, renal, integumentary, and gastrointestinal systems","Fluid and electrolyte imbalances affect membrane excitability and perfusion across all seven major body systems."],["B","Cardiovascular, renal, and integumentary systems exclusively until laboratory values normalize","Limiting assessment to three systems misses early neurological, respiratory, musculoskeletal, and gastrointestinal complications."],["C","Respiratory and neurological systems only when serum osmolality exceeds 300 mOsm/kg","Multi-system monitoring is required continuously for any fluid or electrolyte imbalance regardless of osmolality threshold."],["D","Gastrointestinal and musculoskeletal systems only after intravenous fluid replacement begins","Baseline and ongoing assessment across all seven organ systems is necessary before and during therapy."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"For any fluid or electrolyte imbalance, closely monitor cardiovascular, respiratory, neuro, MSK, renal, integumentary, and GI status.","fc":["Priority Multi-System Monitoring","Cardiovascular, respiratory, and neurological status | Musculoskeletal and renal function | Integumentary and gastrointestinal status","Section II: Maintaining Balance (p. 91)"]},{"i":29,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is examining the integumentary and gastrointestinal systems of a client with prolonged vomiting and poor oral intake. Which findings indicate fluid volume deficit? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Dry skin with poor turgor and tenting","Interstitial fluid depletion reduces skin elasticity, causing dryness and delayed recoil (tenting) when pinched."],["B","Dry oral mucous membranes and intense thirst","Reduced salivary secretion dries the oral mucosa while hypothalamic osmoreceptors trigger thirst."],["C","Decreased gastrointestinal motility with diminished bowel sounds and constipation","Fluid depletion slows peristalsis, producing hypoactive bowel sounds and hard, constipated stools."],["D","Pitting edema in dependent areas with moist skin","Dependent pitting edema indicates excess interstitial fluid volume rather than fluid volume deficit."],["E","Hyperactive bowel sounds with frequent watery diarrhea","Increased GI motility and diarrhea are associated with fluid volume excess rather than dehydration."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Fluid volume deficit causes dry skin, tenting, dry mouth, thirst, diminished bowel sounds, and constipation.","fc":["Fluid Volume Deficit: Skin & GI (Table 8.1)","Dry pale cool skin, poor turgor, tenting | Dry mouth and thirst | Decreased motility, diminished bowel sounds, constipation","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":30,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":3,"st":"A nurse is reviewing four assigned clients at the start of the shift. Which client is at highest risk for developing a fluid volume deficit?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A client with a newly created ileostomy draining liquid stool","Ileostomy drainage bypasses colonic water reabsorption, causing continuous isotonic fluid and electrolyte loss."],["B","A client with chronic heart failure receiving maintenance oral digoxin","Heart failure predisposes clients to fluid retention and fluid volume excess rather than fluid volume deficit."],["C","A client receiving long-term oral corticosteroid therapy for lupus","Long-term corticosteroid therapy promotes sodium and water retention, increasing risk for isotonic overhydration."],["D","A client undergoing frequent wound irrigations with sterile water","Frequent hypotonic wound irrigations risk systemic absorption and hypotonic fluid volume excess."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Clients with ileostomies, GI suctioning, or diuretic therapy lose fluid and face high risk for fluid volume deficit.","fc":["High-Risk Clients: Fluid Volume Deficit","Ileostomy or colostomy drainage | Prolonged vomiting, diarrhea, or GI suctioning | Excessive diuretic therapy","Practice Questions 12-14 (pp. 102, 104)"]},{"i":31,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with poor cardiac function receives inadequately controlled isotonic intravenous fluid therapy and develops isotonic overhydration. Which life-threatening complications does the nurse monitor for closely?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Heart failure and pulmonary edema","Isotonic overhydration expands extracellular volume, causing circulatory overload, heart failure, and pulmonary edema."],["B","Intracellular dehydration and cerebral cell shrinkage","Isotonic overhydration expands only the extracellular compartment without shifting water out of intracellular spaces."],["C","Severe hypernatremia and osmotic demyelination","Isotonic fluids have normal sodium concentration and expand extracellular fluid without causing hypernatremia."],["D","Hypovolemic shock and flat jugular veins","Circulatory overload causes venous distention and hypertension rather than hypovolemic shock."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Isotonic overhydration expands only the extracellular compartment and can precipitate heart failure and pulmonary edema.","fc":["Isotonic Overhydration (Hypervolemia)","Expands only ECF; no shift between ECF and ICF | Causes circulatory overload and interstitial edema | Risk of heart failure and pulmonary edema","Section IV.B: Fluid Volume Excess (p. 92)"]},{"i":32,"t":"m","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client inadvertently receives a rapid infusion of hypertonic saline and develops hypertonic overhydration. How does this rare imbalance alter fluid compartment volumes?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Extracellular fluid volume expands while fluid is drawn out of cells, contracting intracellular volume.","High extracellular sodium pulls water from cells by osmosis, expanding extracellular space and shrinking cells."],["B","Both extracellular and intracellular fluid volumes expand equally due to water intoxication.","Equal expansion of all compartments occurs in hypotonic overhydration rather than hypertonic sodium overload."],["C","Intracellular fluid volume expands while extracellular plasma volume contracts severely.","Fluid shifts out of cells into the hypertonic extracellular compartment, contracting intracellular volume."],["D","Fluid shifts exclusively into the lymphatic system without altering plasma or cellular volumes.","Hypertonic sodium excess directly alters osmotic pressure between plasma and intracellular fluid compartments."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypertonic overhydration (excessive Na intake, hypertonic saline, NaHCO3) expands ECF and contracts ICF.","fc":["Hypertonic Overhydration","Rare; caused by excess Na intake, hypertonic saline, NaHCO3 | Draws fluid out of intracellular compartment | ECF volume expands; ICF volume contracts","Section IV.B-C: Fluid Volume Excess (p. 92)"]},{"i":33,"t":"s","d":1,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is reviewing risk factors for hypotonic overhydration (water intoxication) among hospitalized clients. Which clinical situations can cause hypotonic overhydration? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH)","Excess antidiuretic hormone promotes free water retention, diluting body fluids and expanding all compartments."],["B","Replacing isotonic fluid losses with hypotonic intravenous solutions","Administering hypotonic fluids for isotonic losses dilutes extracellular solutes and drives water into cells."],["C","Irrigating surgical wounds or body cavities with hypotonic fluids","Absorption of hypotonic irrigants adds electrolyte-free water to body compartments, causing water intoxication."],["D","Excessive oral sodium ingestion without water intake","Excessive sodium ingestion causes hypertonic overhydration rather than hypotonic water intoxication."],["E","Untreated diabetes insipidus with polyuria","Diabetes insipidus causes excessive free water excretion and hypertonic dehydration rather than water intoxication."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hypotonic overhydration (water intoxication) expands all fluid compartments and dilutes electrolytes.","fc":["Hypotonic Overhydration (Water Intoxication)","Expands all compartments; dilutes serum electrolytes | Causes: early kidney disease, HF, SIADH | Also caused by hypotonic IV fluids or wound irrigations","Section IV.B-C: Fluid Volume Excess (pp. 92-93)"]},{"i":34,"t":"m","d":5,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A nurse assesses a client with chronic kidney disease and heart failure who reports worsening shortness of breath. Which cardiopulmonary assessment findings confirm fluid volume excess?","ex":{"t":"Vital Signs & Assessment","r":[["Blood Pressure","168/94 mmHg"],["Pulse","112 bpm, bounding"],["Respirations","26 breaths/min, shallow"],["Lung Sounds","Bilateral moist crackles"]]},"o":[["A","Elevated blood pressure, bounding tachycardia, distended neck veins, and moist lung crackles","Intravascular volume overload elevates blood pressure, venous pressure, and pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure."],["B","Orthostatic hypotension, thready pulse, flat neck veins, and clear lung fields","Hypotension, thready pulse, and flat neck veins indicate intravascular fluid volume deficit."],["C","Bradycardia, widened pulse pressure, flat hand veins, and deep Kussmaul respirations","Fluid volume excess produces a bounding rapid pulse, distended hand veins, and shallow tachypnea."],["D","Decreased central venous pressure, dry mucous membranes, and diminished breath sounds","Low central venous pressure and dry mucosa reflect dehydration rather than fluid volume overload."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Fluid volume excess manifests with bounding pulse, hypertension, distended neck/hand veins, high CVP, dyspnea, and moist crackles.","fc":["Fluid Volume Excess: Cardiopulmonary (Table 8.1)","Bounding rapid pulse, elevated BP, high CVP | Distended neck and hand veins | Shallow tachypnea, dyspnea, moist crackles","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":35,"t":"m","d":4,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"The nurse reviews laboratory results for a client admitted with acute fluid overload secondary to syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH). Which laboratory pattern does the nurse expect?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Decreased serum osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, serum sodium, and urine specific gravity","Excess retained water hemodilutes plasma solutes and red blood cells while producing dilute urine."],["B","Increased serum osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, serum sodium, and urine specific gravity","Elevated concentrations of plasma solutes and hematocrit indicate hemoconcentration from fluid volume deficit."],["C","Increased hematocrit and serum sodium with decreased BUN and urine specific gravity","Hemodilution lowers hematocrit, sodium, BUN, osmolality, and urine specific gravity concurrently."],["D","Normal serum osmolality with markedly elevated hematocrit and serum sodium","Water retention reduces serum osmolality and dilutes both hematocrit and serum sodium levels."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Fluid volume excess decreases serum osmolality, hematocrit, BUN, serum sodium, and urine specific gravity via dilution.","fc":["Fluid Volume Excess: Labs (Table 8.1)","Decreased serum osmolality and serum sodium | Decreased hematocrit and BUN (hemodilution) | Decreased urine specific gravity","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":36,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A nurse performs a head-to-toe assessment on a client with severe fluid volume excess. Which neuromuscular, integumentary, and gastrointestinal manifestations are expected? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Altered level of consciousness, headache, and visual disturbances","Cerebral fluid shifts and elevated vascular pressure cause headache, visual changes, and altered consciousness."],["B","Pitting edema in dependent areas with acute weight gain","Excess interstitial fluid accumulates in gravity-dependent tissues and increases total body weight."],["C","Increased gastrointestinal motility with diarrhea, liver enlargement, and ascites","Vascular congestion and bowel wall edema cause liver enlargement, ascites, and hyperactive GI motility."],["D","Tenting of the skin over the sternum with sunken eyeballs","Skin tenting and sunken eyes reflect interstitial fluid depletion from dehydration rather than fluid excess."],["E","Hypoactive bowel sounds with severe constipation","Decreased bowel motility and constipation occur in fluid volume deficit rather than fluid overload."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Fluid volume excess causes altered LOC, headache, visual changes, pitting edema, weight gain, diarrhea, hepatomegaly, and ascites.","fc":["Fluid Volume Excess: Neuro, Skin, GI (Table 8.1)","Altered LOC, headache, visual disturbances, paresthesias | Pitting dependent edema, increased body weight | Diarrhea, liver enlargement, ascites","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":37,"t":"m","d":2,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"A client develops acute fluid volume excess, and the health care provider prescribes diuretic therapy. Which class of diuretic may be prescribed initially to prevent severe electrolyte imbalances?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Osmotic diuretics","Osmotic diuretics may be prescribed initially in fluid volume excess to promote water excretion without severe electrolyte loss."],["B","Potassium-wasting loop diuretics at maximum bolus doses","High-dose loop diuretics rapidly deplete potassium and other electrolytes compared with initial osmotic diuretic therapy."],["C","High-dose thiazide diuretics combined with laxatives","Thiazides promote sodium and potassium wasting and are not specified as initial therapy to prevent electrolyte shifts."],["D","Aldosterone receptor antagonists combined with hypertonic saline","Hypertonic saline worsens fluid volume overload and is contraindicated in fluid volume excess."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"In fluid volume excess, osmotic diuretics may be prescribed initially to prevent severe electrolyte imbalances.","fc":["Fluid Volume Excess: Diuretic Therapy","Administer prescribed diuretics to eliminate excess fluid | Osmotic diuretics may be used initially | Prevents severe electrolyte imbalances during diuresis","Section IV.E: Fluid Volume Excess (p. 93)"]},{"i":38,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":5,"st":"A nurse is implementing the plan of care for a client hospitalized with fluid volume excess due to heart failure. Which nursing interventions are essential for this client?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Restrict fluid and sodium intake, monitor daily weights and intake/output, and track serum electrolytes.","Limiting sodium and water prevents further overload while daily weights and I&O quantify fluid mobilization."],["B","Encourage 3,000 mL of oral fluids daily and weigh the client once weekly.","Increasing fluid intake worsens overload, and weights must be monitored daily rather than weekly."],["C","Administer hypotonic intravenous maintenance fluids and encourage high-sodium broth.","Hypotonic fluids and dietary sodium promote water retention and exacerbate pulmonary congestion."],["D","Discontinue intake and output tracking once oral diuretics are initiated.","Strict intake and output monitoring remains essential throughout diuretic therapy to evaluate fluid balance."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Interventions for fluid volume excess include restricting fluid/sodium, monitoring I&O and daily weights, and checking electrolytes.","fc":["Fluid Volume Excess: Interventions","Restrict fluid and sodium intake as prescribed | Monitor intake/output and daily body weight | Monitor electrolyte values and administer prescribed diuretics","Section IV.E: Fluid Volume Excess (p. 93)"]},{"i":39,"t":"s","d":5,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse reviews assessment findings for a client admitted with acute renal dysfunction. Which cues specifically indicate fluid volume excess rather than fluid volume deficit? Select all that apply.","ex":{"t":"Clinical Assessment Data","r":[["Neck Veins","Distended at 45 degrees"],["Weight Change","+3.2 kg in 48 hours"],["Hematocrit","31% (Baseline 42%)"],["Skin Turgor","2+ pitting ankle edema"],["Bowel Sounds","Hypoactive"]]},"o":[["A","Distended neck veins at 45 degrees","Elevated intravascular volume increases central venous pressure, causing visible jugular venous distention."],["B","Weight gain of 3.2 kg in 48 hours","Rapid weight gain directly reflects acute fluid retention in extracellular tissue spaces."],["C","Hematocrit drop from 42% to 31%","Intravascular water retention hemodilutes red blood cells, lowering the measured hematocrit percentage."],["D","2+ pitting edema in the ankles","Excess fluid shifting into dependent interstitial tissues produces pitting edema."],["E","Hypoactive bowel sounds","Hypoactive bowel sounds occur in fluid volume deficit, whereas fluid excess increases GI motility."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Distended neck veins, rapid weight gain, decreased hematocrit, and pitting edema differentiate fluid excess from deficit.","fc":["Differentiating Fluid Excess vs. Deficit","Excess: distended veins, weight gain, low Hct, pitting edema | Deficit: flat veins, weight loss, high Hct, tenting | Excess increases GI motility; deficit slows motility","Table 8.1: Assessment Findings (p. 93)"]},{"i":40,"t":"m","d":3,"c":7,"j":3,"st":"A nurse reviews the medical records of four clinic clients. Which client does the nurse identify as having the highest risk for developing fluid volume excess?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A client with chronic kidney disease secondary to long-standing diabetes mellitus","Impaired renal filtration and excretion cause sodium and water retention, leading to fluid volume excess."],["B","A client taking daily furosemide who exhibits sternal skin tenting","Loop diuretic therapy with skin tenting indicates active fluid loss and fluid volume deficit."],["C","A client receiving intermittent nasogastric suctioning following bowel surgery","Nasogastric suction removes gastric secretions, placing the client at risk for fluid volume deficit."],["D","A client with high-output drainage from a new surgical ileostomy","Continuous liquid ileostomy output depletes water and electrolytes, causing fluid volume deficit."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Clients with acute kidney injury, chronic kidney disease, and heart failure are at high risk for fluid volume excess.","fc":["High-Risk Clients: Fluid Volume Excess","Acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease | Heart failure | Long-term corticosteroid therapy and excess hypotonic fluids","Section IV & Practice Q14 (pp. 93, 102, 104)"]},{"i":41,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A nurse reviews a client's morning electrolyte panel and notes a serum potassium level of 3.1 mEq/L. How does the nurse interpret this finding?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hypokalemia, which is potentially life-threatening because every body system is affected","Normal serum potassium is 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L, and levels below 3.5 mEq/L threaten all body systems."],["B","Normal serum potassium level requiring only routine daily monitoring","A level of 3.1 mEq/L falls below the normal adult reference range of 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L."],["C","Hyperkalemia requiring immediate administration of sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Hyperkalemia is defined as a serum potassium level exceeding 5.0 mEq/L."],["D","Mild electrolyte variation that affects only gastrointestinal smooth muscle","Potassium deficit affects cardiac, respiratory, neuromuscular, renal, and gastrointestinal systems simultaneously."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Normal serum potassium is 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L; hypokalemia (<3.5 mEq/L) is life-threatening across all body systems.","fc":["Hypokalemia Overview","Normal serum potassium: 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L | Hypokalemia: <3.5 mEq/L | Life-threatening because every body system is affected","Section V.A: Hypokalemia (p. 94)"]},{"i":42,"t":"m","d":2,"c":6,"j":2,"st":"A client with asthma and rheumatoid arthritis reports muscle weakness and leg cramps. Which medications in the client's home regimen contribute to total body potassium loss?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Inhaled albuterol and oral corticosteroids","Excessive use of diuretics, corticosteroids, or inhaled albuterol promotes potassium loss and hypokalemia."],["B","Spironolactone and potassium chloride supplements","Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that retains potassium rather than depleting it."],["C","Calcium carbonate and vitamin D3 supplements","Oral calcium and vitamin D affect calcium balance and do not cause renal potassium wasting."],["D","Aluminum hydroxide and ferrous sulfate","Antacids and iron supplements do not induce total body potassium wasting or hypokalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Medications causing hypokalemia include diuretics, corticosteroids, and inhaled albuterol.","fc":["Medication & Endocrine Causes of Hypokalemia","Diuretics, corticosteroids, and inhaled albuterol | Cushing's syndrome (increased aldosterone) | Vomiting, diarrhea, GI wound drainage, NG suction","Section V.B: Hypokalemia (p. 94)"]},{"i":43,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is reviewing mechanisms of hypokalemia that occur without actual total body potassium loss. Which conditions lower serum potassium by shifting potassium into cells or diluting serum levels? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Metabolic or respiratory alkalosis","Alkalosis drives extracellular potassium ions into cells in exchange for hydrogen ions, lowering serum potassium."],["B","Hyperinsulinism or intravenous insulin administration","Insulin stimulates cellular uptake of potassium from the extracellular fluid into body cells."],["C","Water intoxication and potassium-deficient intravenous therapy","Excessive electrolyte-free water or potassium-free IV fluids dilute the extracellular potassium concentration."],["D","Prolonged nasogastric suctioning","Nasogastric suctioning causes actual physical loss of potassium-rich gastrointestinal fluid from the body."],["E","Metabolic acidosis and massive tissue trauma","Acidosis and tissue destruction shift potassium out of cells into the blood, causing hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Alkalosis and hyperinsulinism shift potassium into cells, while water intoxication and K-free IV fluids dilute serum potassium.","fc":["Non-Loss Causes of Hypokalemia","ICF shift: alkalosis and hyperinsulinism | Dilution: water intoxication, K-deficient IV solutions | Inadequate intake: fasting or NPO status","Section V.B: Hypokalemia (p. 94)"]},{"i":44,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with gastroenteritis has had vomiting and diarrhea for 3 days and reports weakness and leg cramps. Assessment reveals shallow respirations, a thready pulse, and prominent U waves on the cardiac monitor. Which condition do these cues indicate?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hypokalemia","Prolonged GI losses, shallow respirations, thready pulse, leg cramps, and prominent U waves are classic hypokalemia cues."],["B","Hyperkalemia","Hyperkalemia produces diarrhea, tall peaked T waves, and widened QRS complexes rather than prominent U waves."],["C","Hypercalcemia","Hypercalcemia causes constipation, shortened ST segments, and widened T waves rather than prominent U waves."],["D","Hypermagnesemia","Hypermagnesemia produces bradycardia, prolonged PR intervals, and widened QRS complexes without U waves."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Vomiting/diarrhea, muscle weakness/cramps, shallow breathing, thready pulse, and U waves indicate hypokalemia.","fc":["Clinical Judgment: Hypokalemia Cues","GI fluid losses (vomiting, diarrhea, NG suction) | Weakness, leg cramps, shallow respirations, thready pulse | Prominent U wave following each T wave","Clinical Judgment: Analyze Cues (p. 96)"]},{"i":45,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is assessing the neuromuscular and gastrointestinal systems of a client with a serum potassium of 2.8 mEq/L. Which findings does the nurse expect to observe?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Deep tendon hyporeflexia, loss of tactile discrimination, hypoactive bowel sounds, and paralytic ileus","Hypokalemia reduces smooth and skeletal muscle excitability, causing hyporeflexia, sensory changes, and paralytic ileus."],["B","Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes, positive Chvostek's sign, and hyperactive bowel sounds","Hyperreflexia and Chvostek's sign occur in hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia rather than hypokalemia."],["C","Ascending flaccid paralysis accompanied by frequent watery diarrhea","Ascending paralysis and diarrhea are classic manifestations of hyperkalemia rather than potassium deficit."],["D","Spontaneous muscle twitches with hyperactive bowel motility and abdominal cramping","Hypokalemia slows gastrointestinal motility, causing constipation and abdominal distention rather than diarrhea."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypokalemia causes skeletal weakness, hyporeflexia, loss of tactile discrimination, paresthesias, and paralytic ileus.","fc":["Hypokalemia: Neuro & GI (Table 8.2)","Anxiety, lethargy, confusion, coma; hyporeflexia | Skeletal weakness, leg cramps, paresthesias, tactile loss | Hypoactive/absent bowel sounds, paralytic ileus, constipation","Table 8.2: Assessment Findings (p. 94)"]},{"i":46,"t":"s","d":3,"c":7,"j":1,"st":"A nurse reviews a client's electrolyte report showing a serum potassium level of 2.5 mEq/L. Which electrocardiogram (ECG) changes should the nurse monitor for closely? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","ST segment depression","Hypokalemia alters ventricular repolarization, producing characteristic downward depression of the ST segment."],["B","Shallow, flat, or inverted T waves","Decreased extracellular potassium flattens or inverts the T wave on the electrocardiogram."],["C","Prominent U waves","A distinct U wave appearing after the T wave is a hallmark ECG finding in hypokalemia."],["D","Tall, peaked T waves","Tall, peaked T waves are an early electrocardiographic manifestation of hyperkalemia rather than hypokalemia."],["E","Widened QRS complexes","Widened QRS complexes occur in hyperkalemia and hypermagnesemia rather than potassium deficit."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hypokalemia causes ST depression; shallow, flat, or inverted T waves; and prominent U waves on the ECG.","fc":["Hypokalemia ECG Changes (Table 8.3)","ST segment depression | Shallow, flat, or inverted T waves | Prominent U waves","Table 8.3 & Practice Q3 (pp. 94-95, 101, 103)"]},{"i":47,"t":"m","d":2,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client with mild hypokalemia is prescribed oral liquid potassium chloride twice daily. Which instruction should the nurse provide to promote safe administration and adherence?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Dilute the liquid potassium in juice and take it with meals rather than on an empty stomach.","Diluting liquid KCl masks its unpleasant taste, and taking it with food prevents gastric irritation."],["B","Take the undiluted liquid potassium first thing in the morning before eating breakfast.","Taking oral potassium on an empty stomach causes nausea and vomiting and risks gastrointestinal mucosal injury."],["C","Continue taking the supplement even if dark stools or severe abdominal pain develop.","Abdominal pain, distention, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or GI bleeding require holding the drug and notifying the provider."],["D","Double the next dose if you miss a scheduled morning dose of potassium chloride.","Doubling potassium doses risks acute hyperkalemia and life-threatening cardiac dysrhythmias."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Oral potassium should be taken with food (never on an empty stomach), diluted in juice, and stopped if GI bleeding occurs.","fc":["Oral Potassium Administration","Never take on an empty stomach (causes N/V) | Mix liquid KCl with juice or another liquid | Stop and report abdominal pain, distention, or GI bleeding","Section V.D: Hypokalemia (p. 94)"]},{"i":48,"t":"m","d":5,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client with severe hypokalemia (serum potassium 2.4 mEq/L) is prescribed intravenous potassium chloride replacement. Which administration method is required for client safety?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Dilute the potassium in intravenous fluid and infuse slowly via a central line using an infusion pump.","Potassium is never given IV push, IM, or SC; central line infusion via pump prevents infiltration and cardiac arrest."],["B","Administer the potassium chloride as a slow intravenous push bolus over 3 minutes.","Administering intravenous potassium by bolus or IV push can cause immediate fatal cardiac arrest."],["C","Inject the concentrated potassium chloride deep into the ventrogluteal muscle.","Potassium chloride is never administered via intramuscular or subcutaneous routes due to severe tissue necrosis."],["D","Infuse the diluted potassium bag by gravity drip without an electronic infusion device.","An electronic infusion device is mandatory to prevent accidental rapid infusion and lethal hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Potassium is never given IV push, IM, or SC; it is always diluted, infused via pump, and safest via central line.","fc":["IV Potassium Core Safety","NEVER give IV push, IM, or subcutaneous | Always dilute and administer via infusion device | Central line is safest due to infiltration risk","Section V.D & Box 8.2 (pp. 94-95)"]},{"i":49,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"The nurse is preparing an intravenous infusion of potassium chloride for a client with hypokalemia. Which safety precautions from Box 8.2 must the nurse follow? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Ensure dilution does not exceed 1 mEq of potassium per 10 mL of solution.","Limiting concentration to 1 mEq/10 mL prevents severe venous irritation, phlebitis, and accidental bolus toxicity."],["B","Rotate and invert the IV bag before and during infusion to distribute potassium evenly.","Inverting and rotating the bag prevents potassium from pooling at the bottom and infusing as a concentrated bolus."],["C","Maintain the infusion rate at 5 to 10 mEq/hr and never exceed 20 mEq/hr under any circumstances.","Exceeding 20 mEq/hr risks fatal cardiac arrest, and rates above 10 mEq/hr require continuous cardiac monitoring."],["D","Assess renal function before starting and monitor intake and output during the infusion.","Adequate renal function is required to excrete excess potassium and prevent iatrogenic hyperkalemia."],["E","Administer rates up to 15 mEq/hr on a general medical floor without cardiac monitoring.","Any client receiving more than 10 mEq/hr of IV potassium must be placed on continuous cardiac monitoring."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"IV KCl limits: max 1 mEq/10 mL dilution, invert bag, max 5-10 mEq/hr (never >20 mEq/hr), cardiac monitor if >10 mEq/hr.","fc":["IV Potassium Precautions (Box 8.2)","Max dilution: 1 mEq/10 mL; invert/rotate bag | Max rate: 5-10 mEq/hr (never >20 mEq/hr) | Cardiac monitor if >10 mEq/hr; check renal/I&O","Box 8.2: IV Potassium Precautions (p. 95)"]},{"i":50,"t":"m","d":4,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"During an infusion of intravenous potassium chloride at 10 mEq/hr via a peripheral forearm vein, the client reports burning pain and the nurse notes redness and swelling at the site. What is the nurse's priority action?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Stop the intravenous potassium infusion immediately and assess the site.","Potassium irritates veins and causes tissue damage upon infiltration, requiring immediate cessation of the infusion."],["B","Slow the infusion rate to 5 mEq/hr and apply a warm compress over the cannula.","Continuing an irritating potassium infusion into an infiltrated or phlebitic vein risks severe tissue necrosis."],["C","Flush the peripheral intravenous catheter with 10 mL of normal saline to clear the vein.","Flushing an infiltrated line forces concentrated potassium solution further into surrounding subcutaneous tissues."],["D","Reassure the client that burning is expected with potassium and document the finding.","Pain, redness, and swelling signal phlebitis or infiltration and require stopping the infusion immediately."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"If phlebitis or infiltration occurs during an IV potassium infusion, stop the infusion immediately.","fc":["IV Potassium Site Complications","KCl is highly irritating to veins (phlebitis risk) | Inspect IV site frequently for phlebitis or infiltration | Stop infusion immediately if either occurs","Box 8.2: IV Potassium Precautions (p. 95)"]},{"i":51,"t":"m","d":2,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A routine laboratory report shows a serum potassium level of 6.1 mEq/L in an alert client with normal vital signs, normal ECG, and no neuromuscular symptoms. What is the nurse's best initial action?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Prepare to redraw the blood specimen to evaluate for pseudohyperkalemia.","Cell lysis during blood collection causes pseudohyperkalemia, requiring a redraw when clinical symptoms are absent."],["B","Prepare the client for emergent hemodialysis to prevent imminent cardiac arrest.","Emergent dialysis is not indicated for an asymptomatic laboratory artifact without ECG changes until redrawn."],["C","Administer intravenous calcium gluconate immediately via rapid push.","IV calcium is reserved for confirmed severe hyperkalemia with myocardial excitability rather than suspected pseudohyperkalemia."],["D","Initiate a low-potassium diet and withhold all oral fluids indefinitely.","Confirming whether the elevated level reflects specimen hemolysis takes priority before initiating invasive or restrictive treatments."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Pseudohyperkalemia from blood collection cell lysis requires redrawing the specimen when clinical symptoms are absent.","fc":["Pseudohyperkalemia","Caused by blood specimen collection and cell lysis | Suspected when K >5.0 mEq/L without clinical symptoms | Action: redraw and re-evaluate blood specimen","Section VI.A: Hyperkalemia (p. 94)"]},{"i":52,"t":"m","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse is reviewing four clients on a medical unit. Which client is at highest risk for developing true hyperkalemia (serum potassium exceeding 5.0 mEq/L)?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A client with massive traumatic crush injuries and metabolic acidosis","Cellular destruction and acidosis shift large amounts of intracellular potassium into the extracellular blood compartment."],["B","A client with Cushing's syndrome receiving daily hydrocortisone","Excess aldosterone and glucocorticoids in Cushing's syndrome promote renal potassium excretion and hypokalemia."],["C","A client with ulcerative colitis experiencing frequent watery stools","Colitis causes excessive gastrointestinal loss of potassium-rich fluids, predisposing the client to hypokalemia."],["D","A client who has been overusing stimulant laxatives for constipation","Chronic laxative overuse depletes potassium through the gastrointestinal tract, leading to hypokalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hyperkalemia causes include K-sparing diuretics, kidney disease, Addison's disease, tissue trauma/burns, acidosis, and hyperuricemia.","fc":["Causes of Hyperkalemia","Excess intake: K supplements, salt substitutes, rapid IV K | Decreased excretion: K-sparing diuretics, CKD, Addison's | ICF shift: tissue damage/burns, acidosis, hyperuricemia","Section VI.B & Practice Q15 (pp. 95, 102, 105)"]},{"i":53,"t":"s","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is assessing a client whose serum potassium has risen from 5.3 mEq/L to 6.8 mEq/L. Which neuromuscular findings reflect the progression from early to late hyperkalemia? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Early muscle twitches, cramps, and tingling around the mouth, hands, and feet","Early hyperkalemia increases nerve membrane excitability, causing twitches, cramps, and circumoral or extremity paresthesias."],["B","Late profound skeletal muscle weakness with ascending flaccid paralysis","Sustained high extracellular potassium depolarizes membranes, causing ascending paralysis in the arms and legs."],["C","Late respiratory muscle paralysis when potassium reaches lethal levels","Ascending flaccid paralysis eventually involves trunk, head, and respiratory muscles, leading to respiratory failure."],["D","Early deep tendon hyperreflexia with positive Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs","Positive Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs occur in hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia rather than hyperkalemia."],["E","Late severe tetany and tonic-clonic seizures with facial muscle spasms","Late hyperkalemia causes flaccid paralysis rather than tetany or facial spasms."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hyperkalemia causes early twitches, cramps, and paresthesias, progressing to late ascending flaccid paralysis and respiratory failure.","fc":["Hyperkalemia Neuromuscular Progression (Table 8.2)","Early: twitches, cramps, paresthesias (hands, feet, mouth) | Late: profound weakness, ascending flaccid paralysis | Lethal levels: trunk, head, respiratory muscle paralysis","Table 8.2: Assessment Findings (p. 94)"]},{"i":54,"t":"m","d":5,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a serum potassium level of 6.6 mEq/L. Which sequence of electrocardiographic abnormalities does the nurse anticipate on the cardiac monitor?","ex":{"t":"Cardiac Telemetry & Labs","r":[["Serum Potassium","6.6 mEq/L"],["Heart Rate","48 bpm, irregular"],["Blood Pressure","88/54 mmHg"]]},"o":[["A","Tall peaked T waves, flat P waves, prolonged PR intervals, and widened QRS complexes","Hyperkalemia accelerates repolarization and slows conduction, producing peaked T waves, flat P waves, and wide QRS complexes."],["B","ST segment depression, inverted T waves, and prominent U waves","ST depression and prominent U waves are characteristic electrocardiographic findings of hypokalemia."],["C","Prolonged ST segment and prolonged QT interval","Prolonged ST segments and QT intervals occur in hypocalcemia rather than hyperkalemia."],["D","Shortened ST segment, widened T wave, and first-degree heart block","Shortened ST segments and widened T waves are associated with hypercalcemia rather than hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hyperkalemia produces tall peaked T waves, flat P waves, widened QRS complexes, and prolonged PR intervals.","fc":["Hyperkalemia ECG Changes (Table 8.3)","Tall peaked T waves | Flat P waves | Widened QRS complexes and prolonged PR interval","Table 8.3 & Practice Q7 (pp. 94-95, 101, 103)"]},{"i":55,"t":"m","d":4,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client with acute kidney injury has a serum potassium of 7.1 mEq/L and widened QRS complexes on the cardiac monitor. Which prescribed intravenous medication does the nurse prepare first to protect the heart?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Intravenous calcium","IV calcium is administered in severe hyperkalemia to immediately antagonize potassium and avert myocardial excitability."],["B","Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate","Oral cation-exchange resins remove potassium slowly via the bowel and do not immediately stabilize cardiac membranes."],["C","Intravenous potassium-sparing diuretic","Potassium-sparing diuretics retain potassium and are strictly contraindicated in clients with hyperkalemia."],["D","Subcutaneous regular insulin without glucose","Regular insulin is given intravenously alongside hypertonic glucose rather than subcutaneously without glucose."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"In severe hyperkalemia, prepare for IV calcium administration to avert myocardial excitability.","fc":["Hyperkalemia Cardiac Protection","Severe hyperkalemia risks lethal cardiac dysrhythmias | Administer IV calcium to avert myocardial excitability | Stabilizes cardiac membrane while K is shifted/excreted","Section VI.D: Hyperkalemia (p. 95)"]},{"i":56,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"The nurse is implementing collaborative interventions for a client with severe hyperkalemia (serum potassium 6.4 mEq/L). Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Discontinue all intravenous potassium infusions while keeping the IV catheter patent.","Stopping exogenous potassium halts further elevation while maintaining vascular access for emergency medications."],["B","Administer intravenous hypertonic glucose with regular insulin as prescribed.","Insulin paired with hypertonic glucose drives extracellular potassium into cells while preventing hypoglycemia."],["C","Administer sodium polystyrene sulfonate if renal function is impaired.","Sodium polystyrene sulfonate exchanges sodium for potassium in the gastrointestinal tract to promote fecal potassium excretion."],["D","Prepare the client for hemodialysis if potassium levels remain critically elevated.","Dialysis rapidly removes excess potassium from the bloodstream when levels are critically high or refractory."],["E","Administer potassium-excreting diuretics to an anuric client with end-stage renal failure.","Potassium-excreting diuretics are effective only if the client's renal function is not impaired."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Hyperkalemia management stops K intake, shifts K via IV glucose/insulin, and removes K via diuretics, cation resins, or dialysis.","fc":["Hyperkalemia Interventions","Stop IV/oral K; keep IV patent | IV hypertonic glucose + regular insulin shifts K into cells | K-excreting diuretics (if kidneys work), Kayexalate, dialysis","Section VI.D: Hyperkalemia (p. 95)"]},{"i":57,"t":"m","d":2,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"A client with chronic kidney disease and hyperkalemia requires a packed red blood cell transfusion for severe anemia. Which blood product precaution is most important for the nurse to verify?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Request fresh blood rather than older stored blood to prevent further potassium elevation.","Breakdown of older red blood cells in stored blood releases intracellular potassium into the transfused unit."],["B","Infuse the stored blood rapidly over 30 minutes to minimize cellular hemolysis.","Rapid transfusion of stored blood delivers a concentrated potassium load and risks circulatory overload."],["C","Add 20 mEq of potassium chloride to the blood bag to prevent transfusion-induced hypokalemia.","Adding potassium to blood transfusions is unsafe and worsens existing hyperkalemia."],["D","Administer oral potassium supplements immediately before starting the red blood cell transfusion.","Oral potassium supplements must be withheld in any client with hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Clients with potassium imbalances needing transfusions should receive fresh blood because stored blood releases potassium.","fc":["Blood Transfusions & Potassium","Use fresh blood when possible for K-imbalanced clients | Stored blood cells break down over time | Cell lysis releases K and elevates serum potassium","Section VI.D: Hyperkalemia (p. 95)"]},{"i":58,"t":"m","d":4,"c":3,"j":5,"st":"A nurse provides discharge dietary and medication teaching to a client recovering from hyperkalemia who is prescribed spironolactone. Which statement by the client indicates a need for further teaching?","ex":null,"o":[["A","I will season my food with potassium-based salt substitutes instead of table salt.","Salt substitutes contain high amounts of potassium and must be avoided by clients with hyperkalemia."],["B","I will avoid foods that are high in potassium content as listed on my diet sheet.","Restricting dietary potassium intake prevents recurrent hyperkalemia after hospital discharge."],["C","I will have my serum potassium levels checked regularly while taking spironolactone.","Close potassium monitoring is required because spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic."],["D","I will check with my provider before taking any over-the-counter herbal or mineral supplements.","Over-the-counter supplements may contain hidden potassium that elevates serum levels."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Teach clients with hyperkalemia to avoid high-potassium foods and salt substitutes, and monitor K closely on K-sparing diuretics.","fc":["Hyperkalemia Discharge Teaching","Avoid foods high in potassium | Avoid salt substitutes and K-containing substances | Monitor serum K closely on K-sparing diuretics","Section VI.D: Hyperkalemia (p. 95)"]},{"i":59,"t":"s","d":5,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse is comparing gastrointestinal and cardiovascular assessment cues between hypokalemia and hyperkalemia. Which findings are specific to hyperkalemia rather than hypokalemia? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hyperactive bowel sounds with frequent diarrhea","Elevated extracellular potassium increases gastrointestinal smooth muscle motility, producing hyperactive bowel sounds and diarrhea."],["B","Slow, weak, irregular heart rate with decreased blood pressure","Hyperkalemia depresses cardiac conduction, causing bradycardia, irregular rhythm, and hypotension."],["C","Tall peaked T waves and widened QRS complexes on the ECG","Hyperkalemia produces peaked T waves and QRS widening, whereas hypokalemia produces U waves and ST depression."],["D","Hypoactive to absent bowel sounds with paralytic ileus","Decreased gastrointestinal motility and paralytic ileus are classic findings in hypokalemia."],["E","Shallow respirations with a prominent U wave on telemetry","Prominent U waves occur in hypokalemia rather than hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hyperkalemia causes hyperactive bowel sounds, diarrhea, slow weak irregular pulse, peaked T waves, and wide QRS.","fc":["Hyperkalemia vs. Hypokalemia Cues","Hyperkalemia GI: hyperactive bowel sounds, diarrhea | Hypokalemia GI: hypoactive bowel sounds, paralytic ileus | Hyperkalemia CV: slow weak pulse, peaked T, wide QRS","Table 8.2: Assessment Findings (p. 94)"]},{"i":60,"t":"m","d":3,"c":8,"j":3,"st":"A client with end-stage kidney disease has a serum potassium level of 5.7 mEq/L. Which nursing action takes highest priority?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Place the client on continuous cardiac telemetry and assess for peaked T waves and widened QRS complexes.","Potassium imbalances cause life-threatening dysrhythmias and cardiac arrest, making cardiac monitoring the immediate priority."],["B","Encourage the client to ambulate in the hallway to stimulate bowel motility.","Ambulation is unsafe until cardiac rhythm stability is confirmed in a client with hyperkalemia."],["C","Administer an oral potassium supplement with orange juice to prevent muscle cramping.","Administering additional potassium to a hyperkalemic client can precipitate lethal ventricular dysrhythmias."],["D","Obtain a dietary consult to increase fresh fruits and vegetables in the client's meals.","High-potassium fruits and vegetables must be restricted rather than increased in hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Monitor clients with potassium imbalances closely for life-threatening cardiac dysrhythmias leading to cardiac arrest.","fc":["Priority Care in Potassium Imbalance","Potassium imbalance causes life-threatening dysrhythmias | Can rapidly progress to cardiac arrest | Continuous cardiac monitoring is essential","Section VI.C Safety Alert (p. 95)"]},{"i":61,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A nurse reviews four client profiles on a medical-surgical unit. Which client is at highest risk for developing hyponatremia (serum sodium less than 135 mEq/L)?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A client taking daily loop diuretics with excessive diaphoresis","Diuretics and excessive perspiration increase renal and cutaneous sodium excretion, lowering serum sodium below 135 mEq/L."],["B","A client with Cushing's syndrome and elevated cortisol levels","Cushing's syndrome promotes renal sodium retention and increases the risk for hypernatremia."],["C","A client with primary hyperaldosteronism retaining sodium and water","Hyperaldosteronism increases renal sodium reabsorption, leading to hypernatremia rather than hyponatremia."],["D","A client receiving long-term high-dose corticosteroid therapy","Corticosteroids reduce renal sodium excretion and predispose clients to hypernatremia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Normal serum sodium is 135-145 mEq/L; hyponatremia (<135 mEq/L) results from diuretics, diaphoresis, GI losses, or SIADH.","fc":["Hyponatremia Causes","Normal serum sodium: 135 to 145 mEq/L | Excretion: diuretics, diaphoresis, GI loss, Addison's | Dilution: SIADH, heart failure, hyperglycemia, freshwater drowning","Section VII.B & Practice Q8 (pp. 96, 101, 104)"]},{"i":62,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse assesses a client with a serum sodium of 126 mEq/L secondary to prolonged diuretic therapy and dehydration. Which cardiovascular findings does the nurse expect in hypovolemic hyponatremia?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Thready, weak, rapid pulse; hypotension; flat neck veins; and low central venous pressure","When hyponatremia is accompanied by hypovolemia, intravascular depletion causes hypotension, weak tachycardia, and flat veins."],["B","Rapid, bounding pulse; elevated blood pressure; and distended neck veins","Bounding pulse and elevated blood pressure occur when hyponatremia is accompanied by hypervolemia."],["C","Bradycardia with severe hypertension and widened pulse pressure","Hypovolemic hyponatremia lowers blood pressure and triggers compensatory tachycardia rather than bradycardia."],["D","Normal blood pressure with bounding peripheral pulses and elevated central venous pressure","Normovolemic hyponatremia presents with rapid pulse and normal blood pressure without elevated central venous pressure."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Cardiovascular signs in hyponatremia depend on vascular volume: hypovolemia causes hypotension, thready pulse, and flat veins.","fc":["Hyponatremia CV Signs by Volume (Table 8.4)","Normovolemic: rapid pulse, normal BP | Hypovolemic: thready rapid pulse, hypotension, flat veins | Hypervolemic: bounding rapid pulse, normal/high BP","Table 8.4: Assessment Findings (p. 97)"]},{"i":63,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A client with heart failure receiving high doses of a diuretic has flat neck veins, generalized muscle weakness, and diminished deep tendon reflexes. Which additional findings support suspected hyponatremia? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hyperactive bowel sounds with nausea and abdominal cramping","Hyponatremia increases gastrointestinal motility, producing nausea, abdominal cramping, diarrhea, and hyperactive bowel sounds."],["B","Increased urinary output with decreased urinary specific gravity","Reduced extracellular osmolality inhibits ADH release, increasing dilute urine output with low specific gravity."],["C","Headache, personality changes, and confusion","Cerebral cellular swelling from hypo-osmolality causes headache, behavioral changes, confusion, and seizures."],["D","Dry, sticky tongue with extreme thirst and elevated urine specific gravity","Dry sticky mucosa, extreme thirst, and concentrated urine indicate hypernatremia rather than hyponatremia."],["E","Spontaneous muscle twitches with hyperactive deep tendon reflexes","Hyponatremia causes generalized skeletal muscle weakness and diminished deep tendon reflexes rather than hyperreflexia."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hyponatremia causes headache, confusion, weakness, diminished DTRs, hyperactive bowel sounds, and low urine specific gravity.","fc":["Hyponatremia Assessment (Table 8.4)","CNS: headache, personality changes, confusion, seizures | MSK/GI: weakness, diminished DTRs, hyperactive bowel sounds | Renal: increased urine output, low specific gravity","Table 8.4 & Practice Q9 (pp. 97, 101-102, 104)"]},{"i":64,"t":"m","d":4,"c":2,"j":5,"st":"A client is hospitalized with severe symptomatic hyponatremia (serum sodium 116 mEq/L) and is receiving intravenous sodium chloride therapy. Which complication must the nurse prevent by avoiding rapid serum sodium correction?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Osmotic demyelination injury","Correcting severe hyponatremia too rapidly causes sudden fluid shifts out of brain cells, resulting in osmotic demyelination."],["B","Acute pulmonary embolism","Rapid sodium shifts injure central nervous system myelin sheaths rather than causing venous thromboembolism."],["C","Hypocalcemic tetany","Rapid sodium correction does not directly precipitate acute parathyroid-mediated hypocalcemic tetany."],["D","Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest","The primary neurological hazard of overly rapid hyponatremia correction is osmotic demyelination injury."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Correcting severe hyponatremia too quickly places the client at high risk for osmotic demyelination injury.","fc":["Severe Hyponatremia Safety Alert","Hospitalize to monitor sodium and neuro status closely | Avoid rapid serum sodium shifts during correction | Rapid correction causes osmotic demyelination injury","Section VII.D: Hyponatremia (p. 96)"]},{"i":65,"t":"m","d":1,"c":6,"j":3,"st":"A client taking oral lithium carbonate for bipolar disorder develops gastrointestinal illness with vomiting and a serum sodium of 128 mEq/L. Which priority laboratory value must the nurse monitor?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Serum lithium level, because hyponatremia diminishes lithium excretion and precipitates toxicity","Hyponatremia causes the kidneys to retain lithium alongside sodium, rapidly precipitating life-threatening lithium toxicity."],["B","Serum uric acid level, because hyponatremia triggers acute gouty arthritis","Hyponatremia directly impairs renal lithium clearance rather than causing hyperuricemia."],["C","Serum amylase level, because hyponatremia causes acute hemorrhagic pancreatitis","Monitoring serum lithium is critical because low sodium directly precipitates lithium toxicity."],["D","Serum bilirubin level, because hyponatremia induces acute hepatic cholestasis","Lithium retention during hyponatremia is a classic high-alert drug-electrolyte interaction requiring lithium level checks."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hyponatremia diminishes renal lithium excretion and precipitates lithium toxicity in clients taking lithium.","fc":["Hyponatremia & Lithium Toxicity","Monitor serum lithium levels in hyponatremic clients | Hyponatremia diminishes renal lithium excretion | Directly precipitates lithium toxicity","Section VII.D Safety Alert (p. 96)"]},{"i":66,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"The nurse is reviewing tailored medical management plans for clients with different types of hyponatremia. Which interventions match the underlying etiology? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Administer intravenous sodium chloride infusions when hyponatremia is accompanied by hypovolemia.","Isotonic or hypertonic sodium chloride restores both depleted sodium stores and intravascular fluid volume."],["B","Administer osmotic diuretics when hyponatremia is accompanied by fluid volume excess.","Osmotic diuretics promote free water excretion rather than sodium loss in hypervolemic hyponatremia."],["C","Administer medications that antagonize antidiuretic hormone when caused by SIADH.","ADH receptor antagonists block excess antidiuretic hormone activity, allowing renal free water excretion."],["D","Instruct the client to increase dietary sodium intake when prescribed for mild depletion.","Increasing oral sodium intake helps restore serum sodium levels when fluid overload is absent."],["E","Administer large volumes of hypotonic intravenous dextrose water to dilute blood viscosity.","Hypotonic fluids worsen serum sodium dilution and cerebral edema in hyponatremic clients."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Hyponatremia treatment varies by volume status: IV NaCl for hypovolemia, osmotic diuretics for hypervolemia, ADH antagonists for SIADH.","fc":["Hyponatremia Interventions","Hypovolemic: IV sodium chloride infusions | Hypervolemic: osmotic diuretics (excrete water, not Na) | SIADH: medications that antagonize ADH","Section VII.D: Hyponatremia (p. 96)"]},{"i":67,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse reviews the morning electrolyte report showing a serum sodium of 152 mEq/L in an older adult client who is NPO with a high fever. Which pathophysiological mechanisms explain this hypernatremia?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Decreased water intake from NPO status combined with increased insensible water loss from fever","Hypernatremia (>145 mEq/L) develops when water intake drops or insensible water losses exceed sodium loss."],["B","Excessive renal sodium excretion triggered by adrenal insufficiency","Adrenal insufficiency causes sodium wasting and hyponatremia rather than hypernatremia."],["C","Excessive antidiuretic hormone secretion causing free water retention","SIADH causes water retention and dilutional hyponatremia rather than elevated serum sodium."],["D","Overingestion of hypotonic oral fluids during febrile illness","Excessive hypotonic fluid ingestion dilutes serum sodium and causes hyponatremia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypernatremia (>145 mEq/L) results from decreased sodium excretion (Cushing's, steroids), excess Na intake, or water loss/NPO.","fc":["Hypernatremia Causes","Serum sodium >145 mEq/L | Decreased Na excretion: Cushing's, steroids, hyperaldosteronism | Water loss/low intake: NPO, fever, DI, watery diarrhea","Section VIII.B: Hypernatremia (p. 96)"]},{"i":68,"t":"m","d":5,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A client is admitted with a serum sodium of 156 mEq/L and hypovolemia. Which hallmark clinical manifestations does the nurse expect during the physical assessment?","ex":{"t":"Vitals & Labs","r":[["Serum Sodium","156 mEq/L"],["Urine Specific Gravity","1.036"],["Temperature","101.4 F (38.6 C)"]]},"o":[["A","Agitation, confusion, extreme thirst, dry sticky tongue and mucous membranes, and flushed skin","Altered cerebral function is the most common sign of hypernatremia, paired with thirst and dry sticky mucosa."],["B","Lethargy, coma, moist mucous membranes, and excessive salivation","Lethargy and coma occur in hypervolemic hypernatremia, whereas hypovolemic hypernatremia causes agitation and seizures."],["C","Decreased urinary specific gravity with profuse clear polyuria","Hypernatremia stimulates ADH release, producing concentrated urine with elevated specific gravity and oliguria."],["D","Hyperactive bowel sounds with frequent watery diarrhea","Increased bowel motility and diarrhea are manifestations of hyponatremia rather than hypernatremia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Altered cerebral function is the most common sign of hypernatremia, alongside extreme thirst, dry sticky tongue, and flushed skin.","fc":["Hypernatremia Assessment (Table 8.4)","CNS (most common): agitation/seizures (hypovolemic) or coma (hypervolemic) | Extreme thirst; dry, sticky tongue and mucous membranes | Dry flushed skin; high urine specific gravity","Table 8.4: Assessment Findings (p. 97)"]},{"i":69,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is monitoring the neuromuscular status of a client with progressive hypernatremia. Which findings reflect the transition from early to late neuromuscular involvement? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Early spontaneous muscle twitches and irregular muscle contractions","Initial neuronal membrane irritability in hypernatremia produces spontaneous twitches and irregular contractions."],["B","Late generalized skeletal muscle weakness","Continued sodium elevation impairs neuromuscular transmission, resulting in late skeletal muscle weakness."],["C","Late diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes","As skeletal muscle weakness progresses in late hypernatremia, deep tendon reflexes become diminished or absent."],["D","Late hyperactive deep tendon reflexes with positive Trousseau's sign","Hyperactive reflexes and Trousseau's sign occur in hypocalcemia rather than late hypernatremia."],["E","Early ascending flaccid paralysis starting in the lower extremities","Ascending flaccid paralysis is a late manifestation of severe hyperkalemia rather than early hypernatremia."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Hypernatremia produces early muscle twitches/contractions and late skeletal muscle weakness with diminished/absent DTRs.","fc":["Hypernatremia Neuromuscular Progression (Table 8.4)","Early: spontaneous muscle twitches, irregular contractions | Late: skeletal muscle weakness | Late: diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes","Table 8.4: Assessment Findings (p. 97)"]},{"i":70,"t":"m","d":4,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client with type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypernatremia (serum sodium 151 mEq/L) is prescribed an intravenous infusion of 5% dextrose in water (D5W) to lower serum sodium. Which nursing assessment is essential during this infusion?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Monitor blood glucose levels closely because D5W infusion can precipitate hyperglycemia.","Once dextrose is metabolized, D5W provides free water to lower sodium but elevates blood glucose in diabetic clients."],["B","Monitor for acute hypoglycemia because D5W rapidly stimulates endogenous insulin release.","Infusing 5% dextrose delivers exogenous glucose and risks hyperglycemia rather than hypoglycemia."],["C","Restrict all insulin administration until the D5W infusion is completely finished.","Diabetic clients often require insulin coverage while receiving dextrose-containing intravenous fluids."],["D","Administer sodium-promoting diuretics simultaneously with D5W to accelerate fluid loss.","D5W is prescribed when hypernatremia is due to water loss rather than inadequate renal sodium excretion."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"When IV D5W is prescribed to lower serum sodium in hypernatremia, monitor blood glucose closely in clients with diabetes.","fc":["Hypernatremia IV Therapy: D5W","Used to lower serum sodium in select water-loss cases | Provides free water once dextrose is metabolized | Monitor blood glucose closely in diabetic clients","Section VIII.D: Hypernatremia (p. 96)"]},{"i":71,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse reviews the records of four clients. Which client is at highest risk for developing hypocalcemia (serum calcium lower than 9.0 mg/dL)?","ex":null,"o":[["A","A client with lactose intolerance and acute pancreatitis","Lactose intolerance limits dietary calcium intake, while acute pancreatitis binds and lowers ionized serum calcium."],["B","A client with primary hyperparathyroidism and bone metastases","Hyperparathyroidism and metastatic bone destruction increase bone resorption, causing hypercalcemia."],["C","A client taking daily hydrochlorothiazide for essential hypertension","Thiazide diuretics decrease renal calcium excretion and predispose clients to hypercalcemia."],["D","A client immobilized in skeletal traction for 6 weeks","Prolonged immobility accelerates bone demineralization and elevates serum calcium levels."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Normal serum calcium is 9.0-10.5 mg/dL; hypocalcemia (<9.0 mg/dL) results from lactose intolerance, Crohn's, pancreatitis, or parathyroid loss.","fc":["Hypocalcemia Causes","Normal calcium: 9.0 to 10.5 mg/dL (<9.0 = low) | Poor absorption: lactose intolerance, Crohn's, low Vit D, ESRD | Low ionized Ca: pancreatitis, alkalosis, hyperphosphatemia, parathyroidectomy","Section IX.B & Practice Q5 (pp. 97-98, 101, 103)"]},{"i":72,"t":"m","d":3,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A client with cirrhosis has a total serum calcium of 8.2 mg/dL and a serum albumin of 2.0 g/dL. Using the clinical correction formula (`corrected calcium = serum calcium + 0.8[4 - serum albumin]`), how does the nurse interpret this client's calcium status?","ex":{"t":"Serum Chemistry","r":[["Total Calcium","8.2 mg/dL"],["Serum Albumin","2.0 g/dL"],["Ionized Calcium","4.92 mg/dL"]]},"o":[["A","The corrected calcium is 9.8 mg/dL and ionized calcium is normal, indicating true normocalcemia.","Applying the formula `8.2 + 0.8(4 - 2.0) = 9.8 mg/dL` and normal ionized calcium (4.64-5.28) confirms normocalcemia."],["B","The client has severe symptomatic hypocalcemia requiring immediate IV calcium gluconate.","Because calcium binds to albumin, low albumin falsely lowers total calcium while physiologically active ionized calcium remains normal."],["C","The corrected calcium is 6.6 mg/dL, placing the client at high risk for laryngeal stridor.","Subtracting instead of adding the albumin correction factor miscalculates the true corrected serum calcium level."],["D","The client has severe hypercalcemia requiring urgent isotonic saline and calcitonin.","A corrected calcium of 9.8 mg/dL falls squarely within the normal adult reference range of 9.0 to 10.5 mg/dL."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Correct low-albumin calcium using `serum Ca + 0.8(4 - albumin)` or check ionized calcium (normal 4.64 to 5.28).","fc":["Albumin-Corrected & Ionized Calcium","Formula: corrected Ca = serum Ca + 0.8(4 - albumin) | Check ionized calcium if albumin is low | Normal adult ionized calcium: 4.64 to 5.28","Section IX.D: Hypocalcemia (p. 98)"]},{"i":73,"t":"s","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is assessing a client after a total thyroidectomy for early signs of hypocalcemia using Chvostek's and Trousseau's tests. Which assessment techniques and positive findings are accurate? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Lightly tap over the facial nerve in front of the ear to elicit Chvostek's sign.","Tapping the facial nerve anterior to the ear tests neuromuscular irritability of the facial muscles."],["B","Contraction or twitching of ipsilateral facial muscles constitutes a positive Chvostek's sign.","Facial muscle contraction in response to tapping indicates heightened neuromuscular excitability from hypocalcemia."],["C","Inflate a blood pressure cuff on the upper arm above systolic pressure for a few minutes for Trousseau's sign.","Occluding brachial blood flow briefly provokes latent tetany in the distal forearm and hand muscles."],["D","Involuntary carpal spasm (wrist and finger flexion) indicates a positive Trousseau's sign.","Characteristic carpal spasm induced by cuff inflation confirms neuromuscular irritability associated with hypocalcemia."],["E","Extending the client's knee while flexing the hip elicits a positive Trousseau's sign.","Hip flexion with knee extension describes Kernig's sign for meningeal irritation rather than Trousseau's sign."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Positive Chvostek's sign is facial twitching when tapping in front of the ear; positive Trousseau's is carpal spasm with BP cuff inflation.","fc":["Chvostek's & Trousseau's Signs (Fig. 8.3)","Chvostek's: tap facial nerve in front of ear -> facial twitch | Trousseau's: inflate BP cuff above systolic -> carpal spasm | Both indicate neuromuscular irritability (hypocalcemia/hypomagnesemia)","Fig. 8.3: Tests for Hypocalcemia (p. 99)"]},{"i":74,"t":"m","d":5,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A client with Crohn's disease has a serum calcium level of 7.9 mg/dL. Which cluster of clinical and electrocardiographic manifestations does the nurse expect to observe?","ex":{"t":"Electrolyte & ECG Report","r":[["Serum Calcium","7.9 mg/dL"],["ECG Intervals","Prolonged ST & QT"],["Bowel Sounds","Hyperactive"]]},"o":[["A","Paresthesias of lips and limbs, muscle cramps, hyperactive DTRs, diarrhea, and prolonged ST and QT intervals","Hypocalcemia increases neuromuscular and GI excitability while prolonging ventricular repolarization (prolonged ST and QT).],["B","Profound muscle weakness, hypoactive DTRs, constipation, and shortened ST segment","Muscle weakness, constipation, and shortened ST segments are classic signs of hypercalcemia rather than hypocalcemia."],["C","Hypertension, bounding pulses, bradycardia, and widened T waves on the ECG","Hypocalcemia causes hypotension, decreased heart rate, and diminished pulses rather than hypertension."],["D","Decreased gastric motility, paralytic ileus, and prominent U waves on telemetry","Paralytic ileus and prominent U waves indicate hypokalemia rather than low serum calcium."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypocalcemia causes paresthesias (lips, nose, ears, limbs), tetany, hyperactive DTRs, diarrhea, and prolonged ST/QT intervals.","fc":["Hypocalcemia Assessment (Tables 8.3 & 8.5)","Neuro/GI: lip/limb paresthesias, tetany, hyperactive DTRs, diarrhea | CV: hypotension, bradycardia, weak pulses | ECG: prolonged ST segment and prolonged QT interval","Tables 8.3 & 8.5 (pp. 95, 98)"]},{"i":75,"t":"m","d":4,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client develops acute symptomatic hypocalcemia with laryngeal stridor and tetany following parathyroid surgery. Which nursing actions are essential when administering prescribed intravenous calcium?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Warm the calcium solution to body temperature, infuse slowly with cardiac monitoring, and institute seizure precautions.","Warming reduces venous spasm, slow infusion prevents dysrhythmias, and seizure precautions protect against neuromuscular irritability."],["B","Infuse cold calcium chloride rapidly through a small hand vein without cardiac monitoring.","Rapid infusion of cold calcium causes severe venospasm, hypotension, and cardiac dysrhythmias."],["C","Place the client in a high-stimulation hallway room to monitor level of consciousness continuously.","A quiet environment is required to reduce environmental stimuli that could trigger tetany or seizures."],["D","Keep intravenous magnesium sulfate at the bedside as the emergency antidote for tetany.","Keep 10% calcium gluconate at the bedside for acute calcium deficit rather than magnesium sulfate."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Warm IV calcium to body temp, infuse slowly with ECG/site monitoring, maintain a quiet room, seizure precautions, and keep 10% Ca gluconate.","fc":["Acute Hypocalcemia Interventions","Warm IV calcium to body temp; infuse slowly; monitor ECG/site | Quiet environment and seizure precautions | Keep 10% calcium gluconate available at bedside","Section IX.D: Hypocalcemia (p. 98)"]},{"i":76,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"The nurse is reviewing medications prescribed to increase serum calcium levels in a client with chronic hypocalcemia. Which medications help raise serum calcium levels? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Vitamin D supplements","Vitamin D directly enhances calcium absorption from the gastrointestinal tract into the bloodstream."],["B","Aluminum hydroxide","Aluminum hydroxide binds dietary phosphorus, lowering serum phosphorus and reciprocally raising serum calcium."],["C","Oral calcium supplements","Oral calcium increases intestinal calcium availability to restore normal serum calcium levels."],["D","Loop diuretics such as furosemide","Loop diuretics increase renal calcium excretion and are used to treat hypercalcemia rather than hypocalcemia."],["E","Calcitonin and bisphosphonates","Calcitonin and bisphosphonates inhibit bone resorption and lower serum calcium in hypercalcemia."]],"a":["A","B","C"],"obj":"Medications that increase calcium include oral/IV calcium, vitamin D (enhances absorption), and aluminum hydroxide (lowers phosphorus).","fc":["Medications Raising Serum Calcium","Oral or IV calcium supplements | Vitamin D (aids intestinal calcium absorption) | Aluminum hydroxide (reduces phosphorus, raising calcium)","Section IX.D: Hypocalcemia (p. 98)"]},{"i":77,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with metastatic breast cancer and prolonged immobility has a serum calcium level of 12.4 mg/dL. Which mechanism explains this client's hypercalcemia?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Increased bone resorption of calcium due to metastatic tumor destruction and lack of weight-bearing","Malignancy and immobility accelerate osteoclastic bone breakdown, releasing calcium into the bloodstream."],["B","Increased renal excretion of calcium caused by loop diuretic therapy","Loop diuretics promote calcium excretion, whereas hypercalcemia involves decreased excretion or excess bone resorption."],["C","Malabsorption of vitamin D in the small intestine","Poor vitamin D absorption reduces intestinal calcium uptake and causes hypocalcemia."],["D","Excessive secretion of calcitonin from the thyroid gland","Calcitonin inhibits bone resorption and lowers serum calcium rather than causing hypercalcemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypercalcemia (>10.5 mg/dL) results from hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, bone malignancy, immobility, thiazides, lithium, or dehydration.","fc":["Hypercalcemia Causes","Serum calcium >10.5 mg/dL | Bone resorption: hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, cancer, immobility | Low excretion/other: thiazides, lithium, dehydration, excess Ca/Vit D","Section X.B: Hypercalcemia (pp. 98-99)"]},{"i":78,"t":"m","d":4,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse assesses a client admitted with severe hypercalcemia (serum calcium 13.8 mg/dL). Which clinical and electrocardiographic findings does the nurse anticipate?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Profound muscle weakness, hypoactive DTRs, constipation, shortened ST segment, widened T wave, and heart block","Excess calcium depresses neuromuscular and GI excitability while shortening ventricular repolarization and slowing conduction."],["B","Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes, abdominal cramping, diarrhea, and prolonged QT interval","Hyperactive reflexes, diarrhea, and prolonged QT intervals occur in hypocalcemia rather than hypercalcemia."],["C","Hypotension, thready peripheral pulses, and prominent U waves on the cardiac monitor","Hypercalcemia causes increased blood pressure and bounding pulses rather than hypotension and U waves."],["D","Facial muscle twitching when tapping in front of the ear and carpal spasm","Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs reflect neuromuscular irritability in hypocalcemia rather than hypercalcemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypercalcemia causes weakness, hypoactive DTRs, anorexia/constipation, hypertension, late bradycardia, short ST, wide T, and heart block.","fc":["Hypercalcemia Assessment (Tables 8.3 & 8.5)","Neuro/GI: lethargy/coma, weakness, hypoactive DTRs, constipation | CV: early tachycardia/late bradycardia, hypertension, bounding pulses | ECG: shortened ST segment, widened T wave, heart block","Tables 8.3 & 8.5 (pp. 95, 98)"]},{"i":79,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":4,"st":"A nurse is caring for a client admitted with symptomatic hypercalcemia (serum calcium 13.2 mg/dL). Which prescribed interventions should the nurse implement? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Administer isotonic saline infusions intravenously to promote renal calcium excretion.","Isotonic saline restores hydration and enhances glomerular filtration and urinary excretion of calcium."],["B","Discontinue thiazide diuretics and all calcium- or vitamin D-containing supplements.","Stopping calcium-retaining drugs and supplements halts further elevation of serum calcium levels."],["C","Administer prescribed calcitonin, bisphosphonates, or phosphorus to inhibit bone resorption.","These medications block calcium release from bone or bind calcium to lower serum levels."],["D","Monitor for flank pain and strain all urine to detect calcium-based kidney stones.","Hypercalciuria predisposes clients to renal calculi, requiring flank pain monitoring and urine straining."],["E","Encourage a high-dairy diet to prevent bone demineralization during hospitalization.","Clients with hypercalcemia must avoid foods high in calcium to prevent worsening serum levels."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Treat hypercalcemia with isotonic saline, stopping thiazides/Ca/Vit D, bone-resorption inhibitors, urine straining, and dialysis if severe.","fc":["Hypercalcemia Interventions","Isotonic saline IV; stop thiazides, Ca, and Vit D | Calcitonin, bisphosphonates, phosphorus, aspirin/NSAIDs | Strain urine for stones; avoid high-Ca foods; dialysis if severe","Section X.D: Hypercalcemia (p. 99)"]},{"i":80,"t":"m","d":3,"c":2,"j":5,"st":"A client with severe hypercalcemia secondary to hyperparathyroidism asks to walk to the bathroom. Which safety intervention is highest priority for the nurse?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Assist the client with ambulation and move the client slowly and carefully to prevent pathological fractures.","Calcium imbalances weaken bone integrity, placing the client at high risk for pathological fractures during movement."],["B","Encourage brisk independent ambulation to strengthen weight-bearing bones immediately.","Unassisted brisk walking increases fall risk and mechanical stress on demineralized bones."],["C","Restrict the client to strict bed rest without passive range-of-motion exercises.","Complete immobility worsens bone calcium resorption; assisted, gentle mobility is safer."],["D","Apply tight elastic restraints to the lower extremities before assisting the client to stand.","Restraints are unnecessary and unsafe; direct physical assistance and slow movement prevent fractures."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Clients with calcium imbalances are at risk for pathological fractures; move them carefully and slowly and assist with ambulation.","fc":["Calcium Imbalance Fracture Safety","High risk for pathological fractures in hypo/hypercalcemia | Move the client carefully and slowly | Always assist the client with ambulation","Section X.D Safety Alert (p. 99)"]},{"i":81,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A client with a history of chronic alcoholism and poor nutritional intake has a serum magnesium level of 1.3 mEq/L. How does the nurse classify this electrolyte result?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hypomagnesemia, as the normal adult serum magnesium range is 1.8 to 2.6 mEq/L","Serum magnesium below 1.8 mEq/L defines hypomagnesemia, commonly caused by chronic alcoholism and malnutrition."],["B","Normal serum magnesium level for a client with chronic liver disease","Normal serum magnesium is 1.8 to 2.6 mEq/L regardless of underlying alcohol use history."],["C","Hypermagnesemia requiring immediate intravenous calcium gluconate administration","Hypermagnesemia is defined as a serum magnesium level exceeding 2.6 mEq/L."],["D","Borderline elevation caused by excessive intake of magnesium-containing antacids","Magnesium-containing antacids elevate serum magnesium above 2.6 mEq/L rather than lowering it to 1.3 mEq/L."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Normal serum magnesium is 1.8 to 2.6 mEq/L; hypomagnesemia (<1.8 mEq/L) results from alcoholism, malnutrition, diarrhea, or diuretics.","fc":["Hypomagnesemia Overview","Normal serum magnesium: 1.8 to 2.6 mEq/L | Hypomagnesemia: <1.8 mEq/L | Causes: chronic alcoholism, malnutrition, Crohn's, diarrhea, diuretics, sepsis","Section XI.A-B: Hypomagnesemia (p. 99)"]},{"i":82,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A nurse assesses a client with Crohn's disease whose serum magnesium level is 1.2 mEq/L. Which clinical and electrocardiographic manifestations does the nurse expect to find?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Tachycardia, hypertension, hyperreflexia, positive Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, tall T waves, and depressed ST segments","Magnesium deficit increases neuromuscular and cardiac excitability, mimicking hypocalcemia with tall T waves and ST depression."],["B","Bradycardia, hypotension, absent deep tendon reflexes, and prolonged PR intervals","Bradycardia, hypotension, and absent reflexes are classic signs of hypermagnesemia rather than hypomagnesemia."],["C","Hypoactive bowel sounds, paralytic ileus, and prominent U waves on the ECG","Paralytic ileus and prominent U waves indicate hypokalemia rather than low serum magnesium."],["D","Drowsiness progressing to coma with widened QRS complexes","Lethargy progressing to coma and wide QRS complexes occur in severe hypermagnesemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypomagnesemia causes tachycardia, hypertension, hyperreflexia, positive Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs, tall T waves, and ST depression.","fc":["Hypomagnesemia Assessment (Tables 8.3 & 8.6)","CV: tachycardia, hypertension; ECG: tall T waves, depressed ST | Neuro: hyperreflexia, twitches, tetany, seizures | Positive Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs","Tables 8.3 & 8.6 (pp. 95, 100)"]},{"i":83,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A hospitalized client with severe hypomagnesemia is prescribed intravenous magnesium sulfate. Which nursing actions and precautions are essential during treatment? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Initiate seizure precautions and monitor serum magnesium levels frequently.","Severe hypomagnesemia lowers the seizure threshold, requiring seizure precautions and serial magnesium monitoring."],["B","Assess deep tendon reflexes frequently during the infusion to detect early hypermagnesemia.","Diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes are an early warning sign of magnesium toxicity during IV replacement."],["C","Avoid intramuscular magnesium injections because they cause severe pain and tissue damage.","Intravenous administration is preferred in ill clients because intramuscular magnesium causes pain and tissue necrosis."],["D","Monitor and replace coexisting low serum calcium levels as prescribed.","Hypocalcemia frequently accompanies hypomagnesemia and must be corrected concurrently to resolve neuromuscular irritability."],["E","Administer oral magnesium supplements aggressively if the client has active watery diarrhea.","Oral magnesium preparations cause diarrhea and can worsen gastrointestinal magnesium losses."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"For IV MgSO4, use seizure precautions, monitor DTRs for hypermagnesemia, avoid painful IM route, and correct coexisting hypocalcemia.","fc":["Hypomagnesemia Interventions","Correct accompanying hypocalcemia | Avoid oral Mg if diarrhea occurs; avoid IM route (pain/tissue damage) | IV MgSO4: seizure precautions, check serum Mg and DTRs","Section XI.D: Hypomagnesemia (p. 99)"]},{"i":84,"t":"m","d":4,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A client with stage 4 chronic kidney disease reports taking magnesium hydroxide (Milk of Magnesia) daily for constipation and presents with lethargy and slurred speech. Which electrolyte imbalance does the nurse suspect?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hypermagnesemia, caused by impaired renal magnesium excretion combined with magnesium-containing laxatives","Renal insufficiency prevents urinary magnesium clearance, causing toxic accumulation when magnesium antacids or laxatives are ingested."],["B","Hypomagnesemia, caused by accelerated gastrointestinal magnesium loss from laxative use","In renal insufficiency, magnesium-containing laxatives cause systemic retention rather than depletion."],["C","Hypernatremia, caused by excessive sodium absorption from magnesium hydroxide","Magnesium hydroxide delivers a magnesium load rather than sodium and causes hypermagnesemia in renal failure."],["D","Hypophosphatemia, caused by enhanced renal phosphate excretion","Chronic kidney disease impairs phosphate excretion and causes hyperphosphatemia rather than hypophosphatemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypermagnesemia (>2.6 mEq/L) is caused by renal insufficiency, magnesium antacids/laxatives, or excessive IV magnesium.","fc":["Hypermagnesemia Causes","Serum magnesium >2.6 mEq/L | Renal insufficiency (decreased renal excretion) | Mg-containing antacids/laxatives or excess IV Mg","Section XII.A-B: Hypermagnesemia (p. 99)"]},{"i":85,"t":"m","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse assesses a client receiving intravenous magnesium sulfate whose serum magnesium level has reached 4.2 mEq/L. Which clinical and electrocardiographic findings does the nurse expect?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Bradycardia, hypotension, diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes, drowsiness, prolonged PR interval, and widened QRS","Excess magnesium depresses neuromuscular transmission and cardiac conduction, causing hypotension, bradycardia, and hyporeflexia."],["B","Hypertension, tachycardia, hyperactive deep tendon reflexes, and tetany","Hypertension, tachycardia, and hyperreflexia occur in hypomagnesemia rather than magnesium excess."],["C","Positive Chvostek's sign, facial twitching, and shortened ST segments","Chvostek's sign indicates hypocalcemia or hypomagnesemia rather than elevated serum magnesium."],["D","Hyperactive bowel sounds, severe diarrhea, and prominent U waves","Diarrhea and U waves are associated with potassium imbalances rather than hypermagnesemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypermagnesemia causes bradycardia, hypotension, respiratory insufficiency, diminished/absent DTRs, drowsiness, and long PR/wide QRS.","fc":["Hypermagnesemia Assessment (Tables 8.3 & 8.6)","CV: bradycardia, hypotension, cardiac arrest; Resp: insufficiency | Neuro: diminished/absent DTRs, weakness, drowsiness to coma | ECG: prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complexes","Tables 8.3 & 8.6 (pp. 95, 100)"]},{"i":86,"t":"s","d":3,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client receiving intravenous magnesium sulfate develops respiratory depression, absent patellar reflexes, and a serum magnesium level of 4.8 mEq/L. Which emergency and supportive actions should the nurse implement? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Stop the magnesium infusion and prepare to administer intravenous calcium gluconate or calcium chloride.","Calcium gluconate is the specific antidote that directly reverses the toxic cardiac and neuromuscular effects of magnesium."],["B","Prepare to administer prescribed diuretics to enhance renal excretion of excess magnesium.","Diuretics promote urinary clearance of magnesium in clients with adequate kidney function."],["C","Instruct the client to restrict dietary intake of magnesium-containing foods once stable.","Limiting dietary magnesium prevents recurrent elevation after acute toxicity resolves."],["D","Instruct the client to avoid over-the-counter antacids and laxatives containing magnesium.","Avoiding magnesium-based antacids and laxatives prevents exogenous magnesium accumulation."],["E","Administer oral magnesium oxide to bind excess magnesium in the gastrointestinal tract.","Administering additional magnesium worsens toxicity and can precipitate fatal respiratory or cardiac arrest."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Calcium gluconate is the antidote for magnesium overdose; also give diuretics and restrict Mg foods, antacids, and laxatives.","fc":["Hypermagnesemia Interventions","Antidote: IV calcium gluconate (or calcium chloride) | Diuretics to increase renal Mg excretion | Restrict Mg foods; avoid Mg antacids and laxatives","Section XII.D: Hypermagnesemia (p. 100)"]},{"i":87,"t":"m","d":2,"c":6,"j":6,"st":"A nurse administers intravenous calcium gluconate to a client experiencing acute magnesium toxicity. Which assessment finding best indicates that the antidote has been effective?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Return of deep tendon reflexes and improved respiratory rate and depth","Calcium antagonizes magnesium at the neuromuscular junction, restoring deep tendon reflexes and respiratory muscle strength."],["B","Development of sustained carpal spasm when inflating a blood pressure cuff","Carpal spasm indicates overcorrection into symptomatic hypocalcemia rather than balanced neuromuscular recovery."],["C","Decrease in serum sodium level from 142 mEq/L to 132 mEq/L","Calcium gluconate reverses magnesium toxicity without lowering serum sodium concentrations."],["D","Appearance of tall peaked T waves and absent P waves on telemetry","Peaked T waves and absent P waves indicate severe hyperkalemia rather than resolution of magnesium toxicity."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Effective IV calcium gluconate reverses magnesium's depressant effects on cardiac and respiratory skeletal muscles.","fc":["Evaluating Calcium Antidote in Mg Toxicity","Reverses magnesium effects on cardiac and skeletal muscle | Improved heart rate, blood pressure, and respiratory effort | Return of diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes","Section XII.D & Table 8.6 (p. 100)"]},{"i":88,"t":"m","d":4,"c":7,"j":2,"st":"A nurse is reviewing telemetry strips for four clients with different electrolyte imbalances. Which pairing of electrolyte imbalance and characteristic ECG abnormality is accurate?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Hypercalcemia: shortened ST segment, widened T wave, and heart block","Elevated serum calcium accelerates ventricular repolarization, producing a shortened ST segment, widened T wave, and heart block."],["B","Hypocalcemia: shortened ST segment and tall peaked T waves","Hypocalcemia prolongs the ST segment and QT interval rather than shortening the ST segment."],["C","Hypomagnesemia: prolonged PR interval and widened QRS complexes","Prolonged PR intervals and widened QRS complexes occur in hypermagnesemia rather than hypomagnesemia."],["D","Hyperkalemia: ST segment depression and prominent U waves","ST depression and prominent U waves are characteristic of hypokalemia rather than hyperkalemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"ECG patterns: HypoCa (long ST/QT), HyperCa (short ST, wide T, heart block), HypoMg (tall T, depressed ST), HyperMg (long PR, wide QRS).","fc":["ECG Changes Across Electrolytes (Table 8.3)","HypoCa: prolonged ST & QT | HyperCa: shortened ST, widened T, heart block | HypoMg: tall T, depressed ST; HyperMg: long PR, wide QRS","Table 8.3: ECG Changes (p. 95)"]},{"i":89,"t":"s","d":5,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with chronic alcoholism and acute pancreatitis presents with muscle twitching, positive Trousseau's sign, tachycardia, and hypertension. Laboratory results show magnesium 1.1 mEq/L and calcium 7.8 mg/dL. Which clinical interpretations are accurate? Select all that apply.","ex":{"t":"Admission Labs","r":[["Serum Magnesium","1.1 mEq/L"],["Serum Calcium","7.8 mg/dL"],["Serum Potassium","3.9 mEq/L"]]},"o":[["A","Hypocalcemia frequently accompanies hypomagnesemia and contributes to the client's neuromuscular tetany.","Low magnesium impairs parathyroid hormone release and action, causing concurrent hypocalcemia and tetany."],["B","Both hypomagnesemia and hypocalcemia produce positive Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs.","Neuromuscular irritability from either low magnesium or low calcium triggers facial and carpal spasms."],["C","Interventions must aim to restore both serum magnesium and serum calcium levels concurrently.","Treating hypomagnesemia alone without addressing coexisting hypocalcemia fails to fully resolve neuromuscular irritability."],["D","Tachycardia and hypertension are consistent with the cardiovascular effects of hypomagnesemia.","Unlike hypermagnesemia which causes hypotension and bradycardia, hypomagnesemia causes tachycardia and hypertension."],["E","The client's symptoms are primarily caused by hyperkalemia requiring immediate dialysis.","Serum potassium is normal at 3.9 mEq/L, and the symptoms reflect combined magnesium and calcium deficits."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Hypocalcemia frequently accompanies hypomagnesemia; both cause positive Trousseau's/Chvostek's signs and require dual replacement.","fc":["Coexisting Hypomagnesemia & Hypocalcemia","Hypocalcemia frequently accompanies hypomagnesemia | Both produce hyperreflexia, tetany, Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs | Treatment must restore both Mg and Ca levels","Section XI.D & Table 8.6 (pp. 99-100)"]},{"i":90,"t":"m","d":3,"c":3,"j":5,"st":"A nurse is providing discharge education to a client with chronic kidney disease about safe over-the-counter medication use. Which client statement demonstrates effective teaching?","ex":null,"o":[["A","I will avoid over-the-counter antacids, laxatives, and enemas that contain magnesium or phosphate.","Impaired kidneys cannot excrete magnesium or phosphorus, causing life-threatening hypermagnesemia and hyperphosphatemia."],["B","I can safely use Milk of Magnesia and Fleet enemas whenever I feel constipated.","Magnesium-based laxatives and phosphate enemas cause severe electrolyte toxicity in clients with kidney disease."],["C","I will take aluminum hydroxide on an empty stomach at bedtime to prevent kidney stones.","Phosphate-binding medications must be taken with or immediately after meals to bind dietary phosphorus."],["D","I will use potassium-containing salt substitutes to keep my blood pressure controlled.","Clients with kidney disease retain potassium and must strictly avoid potassium-containing salt substitutes."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Clients with renal insufficiency must avoid magnesium- and phosphate-containing antacids, laxatives, and enemas.","fc":["CKD OTC Medication Safety","Avoid magnesium-containing antacids and laxatives | Avoid phosphate-containing laxatives and enemas | Avoid potassium-containing salt substitutes","Sections VI, XII, & XIV (pp. 95, 99-101)"]},{"i":91,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"A nurse reviews a client's laboratory panel and notes a serum phosphorus level of 2.1 mg/dL alongside a serum calcium level of 10.9 mg/dL. Which physiological principle explains this pairing?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Phosphorus and calcium have a reciprocal relationship, so a drop in phosphorus is accompanied by a rise in calcium.","Normal phosphorus is 3.0 to 4.5 mg/dL, and serum phosphorus and calcium move in opposite directions."],["B","Phosphorus and calcium have a direct relationship, so both electrolytes always decrease together.","Phosphorus and calcium exhibit an inverse reciprocal relationship rather than rising or falling together."],["C","Elevated serum calcium causes the kidneys to retain phosphorus in the proximal tubule.","High calcium typically coincides with low phosphorus due to their reciprocal physiological balance."],["D","A serum phosphorus of 2.1 mg/dL is within the normal range and unrelated to calcium.","Normal adult serum phosphorus is 3.0 to 4.5 mg/dL, making 2.1 mg/dL hypophosphatemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Normal serum phosphorus is 3.0-4.5 mg/dL; phosphorus and calcium have a reciprocal relationship (as one drops, the other rises).","fc":["Phosphorus-Calcium Reciprocal Relationship","Normal serum phosphorus: 3.0 to 4.5 mg/dL | Decrease in phosphorus -> increase in calcium | Increase in phosphorus -> decrease in calcium","Section XIII.A Safety Alert (p. 100)"]},{"i":92,"t":"m","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client hospitalized for severe malnutrition and alcohol withdrawal has a serum phosphorus level of 1.8 mg/dL. Which additional clinical factor could contribute to this client's hypophosphatemia?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Frequent use of magnesium-based or aluminum hydroxide-based antacids","Magnesium and aluminum antacids bind phosphorus in the gastrointestinal tract and increase phosphorus excretion."],["B","End-stage renal insufficiency with oliguria","Renal insufficiency impairs phosphate excretion and causes hyperphosphatemia rather than hypophosphatemia."],["C","Tumor lysis syndrome following chemotherapy initiation","Tumor lysis syndrome releases intracellular phosphate from destroyed cancer cells, causing hyperphosphatemia."],["D","Hypoparathyroidism following total thyroidectomy","Hypoparathyroidism reduces renal phosphate excretion and causes hyperphosphatemia rather than hypophosphatemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Hypophosphatemia (<3.0 mg/dL) results from malnutrition, hyperparathyroidism, malignancy, Mg/Al antacids, hyperglycemia, or respiratory alkalosis.","fc":["Causes of Hypophosphatemia","Serum phosphorus <3.0 mg/dL | Malnutrition/starvation; Mg- or Al-hydroxide antacids | Hyperparathyroidism, malignancy, hyperglycemia, respiratory alkalosis","Section XIII.B & Practice Q10 (pp. 100, 102, 104)"]},{"i":93,"t":"s","d":1,"c":8,"j":1,"st":"The nurse is assessing a client whose serum phosphorus level has dropped from 2.7 mg/dL to 0.9 mg/dL. Which clinical principles apply to hypophosphatemia presentation? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Clients with mild hypophosphatemia may remain completely asymptomatic.","Symptoms of hypophosphatemia often do not appear until serum phosphorus drops to very low levels."],["B","Critical manifestations emerge when serum phosphorus falls below 1.0 mg/dL.","Levels below 1.0 mg/dL produce severe neuromuscular, cardiopulmonary, and skeletal symptoms requiring IV replacement."],["C","Clinical manifestations mirror those of hypercalcemia due to the reciprocal calcium elevation.","Because low phosphorus raises serum calcium, assessment findings overlap with those listed in Table 8.5."],["D","Clients are at increased risk for pathological fractures and require gentle handling.","Phosphorus depletion weakens bone mineralization, necessitating careful movement and fracture monitoring."],["E","Mild hypophosphatemia immediately triggers severe carpal spasm and positive Chvostek's sign.","Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs occur in hypocalcemia (hyperphosphatemia) rather than mild hypophosphatemia."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Mild hypophosphatemia may be asymptomatic; severe levels (<1 mg/dL) cause critical symptoms and pathological fracture risk.","fc":["Hypophosphatemia Clinical Presentation","Mild hypophosphatemia may have no symptoms | Critical manifestations appear when phosphorus <1 mg/dL | Mirrors hypercalcemia; high pathological fracture risk","Section XIII.C-D: Hypophosphatemia (p. 100)"]},{"i":94,"t":"m","d":5,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client with severe malnutrition has a critical serum phosphorus level of 0.8 mg/dL and profound skeletal muscle weakness. Which safety precaution is highest priority before and during prescribed intravenous phosphorus replacement?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Assess the renal system before administering and infuse IV phosphorus slowly to prevent hyperphosphatemia.","Impaired kidneys cannot clear excess phosphorus, and rapid infusion risks severe hyperphosphatemia and secondary hypocalcemia."],["B","Administer intravenous phosphorus as a rapid 5-minute bolus to restore respiratory muscle strength.","Rapid IV phosphorus administration precipitates acute hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemic tetany, and cardiac arrest."],["C","Administer high-dose calcium gluconate through the same intravenous line simultaneously.","Mixing calcium and phosphorus in the same IV line causes dangerous calcium-phosphate precipitation."],["D","Keep the client on strict bed rest without turning to prevent cardiac dysrhythmias.","While gentle handling prevents fractures, assessing renal function and infusing slowly are top medication priorities."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"When giving IV phosphorus for levels <1 mg/dL, assess renal function first and infuse slowly to avoid hyperphosphatemia.","fc":["IV Phosphorus Safety","Indicated when phosphorus <1 mg/dL with critical symptoms | Assess the renal system before administering | Administer IV phosphorus slowly (avoids hyperphosphatemia)","Section XIII.D: Hypophosphatemia (p. 100)"]},{"i":95,"t":"m","d":4,"c":3,"j":5,"st":"A nurse provides discharge dietary and medication teaching to a client recovering from hypophosphatemia. Which instruction should the nurse include in the teaching plan?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Take prescribed oral phosphorus with a vitamin D supplement, increase phosphorus-rich foods, and decrease calcium-rich foods.","Vitamin D aids intestinal absorption, while increasing dietary phosphorus and limiting calcium restores reciprocal balance."],["B","Increase intake of milk, yogurt, and calcium supplements while avoiding phosphorus-rich foods.","High calcium intake lowers phosphorus further due to the reciprocal relationship between calcium and phosphorus."],["C","Resume taking aluminum hydroxide antacids before meals to protect the stomach lining.","Aluminum hydroxide binds phosphorus in the gut and must be discontinued in hypophosphatemia."],["D","Take oral phosphorus on an empty stomach with a calcium-fortified antacid.","Calcium-containing antacids bind phosphorus in the intestine and block its absorption."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Treat mild hypophosphatemia by stopping offending drugs, giving oral phosphorus with vitamin D, and increasing high-P/low-Ca foods.","fc":["Hypophosphatemia Discharge Teaching","Discontinue contributing medications (Mg/Al antacids) | Administer oral phosphorus with vitamin D supplement | Increase high-phosphorus foods; decrease high-calcium foods","Section XIII.D: Hypophosphatemia (p. 100)"]},{"i":96,"t":"s","d":3,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"The nurse is reviewing risk factors for hyperphosphatemia (serum phosphorus exceeding 4.5 mg/dL). Which clinical conditions can cause hyperphosphatemia? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Chronic renal insufficiency with decreased phosphate excretion","Failing kidneys cannot excrete excess dietary phosphorus, making renal insufficiency the most common cause."],["B","Tumor lysis syndrome following cytotoxic chemotherapy","Rapid destruction of malignant cells releases large amounts of intracellular phosphorus into the bloodstream."],["C","Overuse of phosphate-containing laxatives or enemas","Excessive absorption of phosphate from bowel preparations elevates serum phosphorus above 4.5 mg/dL."],["D","Hypoparathyroidism following neck surgery","Deficient parathyroid hormone reduces renal phosphorus excretion and increases serum phosphorus."],["E","Excessive use of aluminum hydroxide antacids","Aluminum hydroxide binds intestinal phosphorus and causes hypophosphatemia rather than hyperphosphatemia."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Hyperphosphatemia (>4.5 mg/dL) results from renal insufficiency, tumor lysis syndrome, phosphate laxatives/enemas, or hypoparathyroidism.","fc":["Causes of Hyperphosphatemia","Serum phosphorus >4.5 mg/dL | Renal insufficiency and hypoparathyroidism | Tumor lysis syndrome and phosphate laxatives/enemas","Section XIV.A-B: Hyperphosphatemia (pp. 100-101)"]},{"i":97,"t":"m","d":2,"c":8,"j":2,"st":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a serum phosphorus level of 6.8 mg/dL and a serum calcium level of 7.6 mg/dL. What is the primary clinical concern associated with hyperphosphatemia?","ex":null,"o":[["A","Clinical complications stem from the secondary hypocalcemia that develops when serum phosphorus rises.","Most body systems tolerate elevated phosphorus well, so clinical manifestations center on resulting hypocalcemia."],["B","Direct neurotoxicity from phosphorus causes immediate ascending flaccid paralysis.","Ascending flaccid paralysis is caused by hyperkalemia rather than elevated serum phosphorus."],["C","Elevated phosphorus directly causes severe hypercalcemia and pathological fractures.","Elevated phosphorus reciprocally lowers serum calcium rather than causing hypercalcemia."],["D","Hyperphosphatemia causes profound osmotic diuresis and severe hypernatremia.","Hyperphosphatemia does not cause osmotic diuresis; clinical problems center on hypocalcemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Most body systems tolerate high phosphorus well; clinical problems in hyperphosphatemia center on resulting hypocalcemia.","fc":["Hyperphosphatemia Pathophysiology","Most body systems tolerate elevated phosphorus well | Rise in phosphorus causes reciprocal drop in calcium | Clinical problems center on resulting hypocalcemia","Section XIV.A: Hyperphosphatemia (p. 100)"]},{"i":98,"t":"m","d":4,"c":6,"j":5,"st":"A client with stage 5 chronic kidney disease is prescribed oral phosphate-binding medication to treat hyperphosphatemia. When should the nurse instruct the client to take this medication?","ex":null,"o":[["A","With meals or immediately after meals so it binds phosphorus from food in the gastrointestinal tract","Phosphate binders must contact ingested food in the stomach and intestine to increase fecal phosphorus excretion."],["B","First thing in the morning at least 2 hours before eating breakfast","Taking phosphate binders on an empty stomach prevents them from binding dietary phosphorus in food."],["C","At bedtime with a phosphate-containing laxative to accelerate bowel elimination","Phosphate-containing laxatives worsen hyperphosphatemia and are contraindicated."],["D","Only when the client experiences tingling around the mouth or muscle cramps","Phosphate binders are taken routinely with every meal to prevent hyperphosphatemia and secondary hypocalcemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Administer phosphate-binding medications with meals or immediately after meals to bind dietary phosphorus for fecal excretion.","fc":["Phosphate-Binding Medication Timing","Increases fecal excretion of phosphorus from food | Must be taken WITH meals or IMMEDIATELY AFTER meals | Manages hyperphosphatemia in renal insufficiency","Section XIV.D: Hyperphosphatemia (p. 101)"]},{"i":99,"t":"s","d":5,"c":3,"j":5,"st":"A nurse develops a comprehensive discharge teaching plan for a client with chronic kidney disease and hyperphosphatemia. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","ex":null,"o":[["A","Take prescribed phosphate-binding medications with meals or immediately after meals.","Timing phosphate binders with food ensures dietary phosphorus is bound and excreted in the stool."],["B","Decrease dietary intake of foods that are high in phosphorus.","Restricting high-phosphorus foods reduces exogenous phosphate absorption when renal clearance is impaired."],["C","Strictly avoid over-the-counter laxatives and enemas containing phosphate.","Phosphate-containing bowel preparations cause rapid, dangerous spikes in serum phosphorus."],["D","Report tingling around the mouth, numbness in fingers, or muscle cramps immediately.","Because hyperphosphatemia causes secondary hypocalcemia, neuromuscular tetany symptoms require prompt evaluation."],["E","Increase daily intake of phosphate-rich dairy products to prevent bone fractures.","High-phosphorus foods must be restricted rather than increased in clients with hyperphosphatemia."]],"a":["A","B","C","D"],"obj":"Hyperphosphatemia care includes taking binders with meals, avoiding phosphate laxatives/enemas, low-P diet, and monitoring hypocalcemia.","fc":["Hyperphosphatemia Discharge Plan","Take phosphate binders with or right after meals | Decrease high-phosphorus foods; avoid phosphate laxatives/enemas | Monitor and manage secondary hypocalcemia","Section XIV.D: Hyperphosphatemia (p. 101)"]},{"i":100,"t":"m","d":3,"c":8,"j":6,"st":"A nurse evaluates a client after 2 weeks of dietary phosphorus restriction and mealtime phosphate-binder therapy for hyperphosphatemia. Which assessment findings confirm successful treatment outcomes?","ex":{"t":"Follow-Up Evaluation","r":[["Serum Phosphorus","3.8 mg/dL"],["Serum Calcium","9.4 mg/dL"],["Chvostek's Sign","Negative"]]},"o":[["A","Serum phosphorus 3.8 mg/dL, serum calcium 9.4 mg/dL, and negative Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs","Normalized phosphorus (3.0-4.5 mg/dL) and calcium (9.0-10.5 mg/dL) without neuromuscular irritability confirm effective therapy."],["B","Serum phosphorus 5.4 mg/dL, serum calcium 8.1 mg/dL, and positive Trousseau's sign","Persistent hyperphosphatemia with secondary hypocalcemia and carpal spasm indicates ineffective management."],["C","Serum phosphorus 1.9 mg/dL, serum calcium 11.2 mg/dL, and profound muscle weakness","Over-suppression of phosphorus has caused hypophosphatemia and reciprocal hypercalcemia."],["D","Serum phosphorus 4.9 mg/dL, prolonged QT interval, and circumoral paresthesias","Elevated phosphorus with prolonged QT interval and paresthesias indicates unresolved secondary hypocalcemia."]],"a":["A"],"obj":"Successful hyperphosphatemia management restores phosphorus (3.0-4.5 mg/dL) and calcium (9.0-10.5 mg/dL) without hypocalcemic tetany.","fc":["Evaluating Hyperphosphatemia Outcomes","Serum phosphorus returns to 3.0 to 4.5 mg/dL | Serum calcium normalizes to 9.0 to 10.5 mg/dL | Absence of Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs and QT prolongation","Sections IX, XIII, & XIV (pp. 97-101)"]}]}
